// Class 11 Physics Unit IV: Work, Energy and Power (06 Marks)
// Official JKBOSE / CBSE / NCERT Syllabus Alignment
// Gold Standard Reference Textbook & 3-Tab Architecture (Pradeep's / S.L. Arora Standard)

export const c11Phy4HtmlOverview = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- CHAPTER TITLE BANNER -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h1 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 24px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Unit IV: Work, Energy and Power (06 Marks)\n    </h1>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Comprehensive Reference Textbook Guide &bull; JKBOSE / CBSE Official Syllabus Alignment\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY & BASIC DEFINITIONS CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      ⚡ Quick Glossary & Fundamental Operational Quantities\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">Work (W):</b> Scalar product of force and displacement; W = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{s} = F s cos\theta. SI Unit: <b>Joule (J)</b> &bull; Dimensions: <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">Kinetic Energy (K):</b> Energy possessed by virtue of translational velocity; K = \frac{1}{2}mv<sup>2</sup> = \frac{p<sup>2</sup>}{2m}. SI Unit: <b>Joule (J)</b> &bull; Dimensions: <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">Power (P):</b> Time rate of doing mechanical work; P = \frac{dW}{dt} = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{v}. SI Unit: <b>Watt (W = J s<sup>-1</sup>)</b> &bull; Dimensions: <b>[M L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">Restitution (e):</b> Ratio of relative speed of separation to approach; e = \frac{v_2 - v_1}{u_1 - u_2}. <b>Dimensionless & Unitless [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    1. Work Done by a Constant Force & a Variable Force\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    In everyday language, the term \"work\" is applied loosely to any mental or physical exertion—such as reading a textbook, standing holding a heavy briefcase, or pushing against a concrete wall. However, in scientific physics, <b>work has a rigorous operational definition</b>. Work is said to be performed only when an applied force succeeds in producing a spatial displacement of the body along or opposite to the line of action of the force.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #00c6ff; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 16px;\">Mechanical Work Done (W)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Work done by a constant force acting on a body is defined as the scalar (dot) product of the force vector and the displacement vector.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Joule (J = N m = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    W = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{s} = F s cos\theta\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    where F = |\u000bec{F}| is the magnitude of the force, s = |\u000bec{s}| is the magnitude of displacement, and \theta is the angle included between the force and displacement vectors (0 le \theta le pi).\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Classification of Work Done:</b>\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">1. Positive Work (0<sup>c</sup>irc le \theta < 90<sup>c</sup>irc):</b> When the force vector has a component in the direction of displacement (cos\theta > 0). <i>Examples:</i> Work done by gravity on a freely falling stone (\theta = 0<sup>c</sup>irc); work done by an engine pulling a train along a straight horizontal track.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">2. Negative Work (90<sup>c</sup>irc < \theta le 180<sup>c</sup>irc):</b> When the force vector opposes the direction of displacement (cos\theta < 0). <i>Examples:</i> Work done by friction on a braking car (\theta = 180<sup>c</sup>irc, W = -f s); work done by gravity on a ball thrown vertically upwards (\theta = 180<sup>c</sup>irc, W = -mgh).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">3. Zero Work (W = 0):</b> Work vanishes under any of the following three distinct physical conditions:\n      <ul>\n        <li>Displacement is zero (s = 0): A person pushing against a stationary brick wall performs zero work, despite experiencing muscular fatigue.</li>\n        <li>Force is zero (F = 0): An isolated particle gliding through deep interstellar space at constant velocity performs zero work.</li>\n        <li>Force is strictly perpendicular to displacement (\theta = 90<sup>c</sup>irc, cos 90<sup>c</sup>irc = 0): A coolie carrying a heavy luggage load on his head while walking along a horizontal railway platform does zero work against gravity because the downward gravitational force mg is at 90<sup>c</sup>irc to horizontal displacement. Similarly, the central gravitational pull of Earth on an orbiting satellite in a circular orbit does zero work.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Work Done by a Variable Force:</b><br>\n    In real physical systems, forces rarely remain constant; they vary continuously with position (such as a stretching spring, gravitational pull across astronomical distances, or electrostatic repulsion).<br>\n    To calculate work done by a variable one-dimensional force F(x), we subdivide the total displacement from x_1 to x_2 into an infinite number of infinitesimal steps dx. Over each tiny step, the force can be treated as effectively constant. The infinitesimal work is dW = F(x) , dx.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 15.5px;\">Calculus Integration for Variable Force</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Infinitesimal Work Element</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">For an infinitesimal displacement dx, work is dW = F(x) , dx.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Definite Integral Formulation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Total work done as position changes from x_1 to x_2 is the definite integral:\n          <br><div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin: 6px 0;\">\n            W = int_{x_1}<sup>x_2</sup> F(x) , dx\n          </div></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Geometric Area Interpretation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">The definite integral \\int_{x_1}<sup>x_2</sup> F(x) dx represents the <b>geometric area under the Force-Displacement (F-x) curve</b> bounded by the displacement axis between limits x_1 and x_2.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">General Three-Dimensional Form</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">In 3D Cartesian coordinates:\n          <br>W = int_{\u000bec{r}_1}<sup>\u000bec{r</sup>_2} \u000bec{F} cdot d\u000bec{r} = int_{x_1}<sup>x_2</sup> F_x , dx + int_{y_1}<sup>y_2</sup> F_y , dy + int_{z_1}<sup>z_2</sup> F_z , dz.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Units of Work & Energy:</b><br>\n    &bull; <b>SI Absolute Unit:</b> Joule (J). 1\text{ J} = 1\text{ N} \times 1\text{ m} = 1\text{ kg m}<sup>2</sup>\text{ s}<sup>-2</sup>.<br>\n    &bull; <b>CGS Unit:</b> Erg. 1\text{ erg} = 1\text{ dyne} \times 1\text{ cm} = 1\text{ g cm}<sup>2</sup>\text{ s}<sup>-2</sup>.<br>\n    &bull; <b>Conversion:</b> 1\text{ J} = (10<sup>5</sup>\text{ dynes}) \times (10<sup>2</sup>\text{ cm}) = mathbf{10<sup>7</sup>\text{ erg}}.<br>\n    &bull; <b>Atomic Unit:</b> Electron-Volt (eV). 1\text{ eV} = 1.602 \times 10<sup>-19</sup>\text{ J}.<br>\n    &bull; <b>Commercial Unit:</b> Kilowatt-hour (kWh). 1\text{ kWh} = (1000\text{ W}) \times (3600\text{ s}) = mathbf{3.6 \times 10<sup>6</sup>\text{ J} = 3.6\text{ MJ}}.\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    2. Kinetic Energy & The Work-Energy Theorem\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    <b>Kinetic Energy (K)</b> is the energy possessed by an object by virtue of its translational motion. A bullet fired from a rifle, a blowing gale of wind turning a turbine, or a rushing river torrent all perform mechanical work by virtue of their velocity.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #00c6ff; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 16px;\">Kinetic Energy (K)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The kinetic energy of a body of mass m moving with velocity v is defined as the total mechanical work required to accelerate the body from a state of rest to that velocity.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Joule (J)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    K = \frac{1}{2} m v<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2} m (\u000bec{v} cdot \u000bec{v})\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Relation Between Kinetic Energy and Linear Momentum:</b><br>\n    Since momentum magnitude is p = mv, multiplying numerator and denominator by m gives:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #80DEEA;\">\n    <b>K = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">p<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2 m</span>\n  </span> quad Longleftrightarrow quad p = sqrt{2 m K}</b>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #00c6ff; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 16px;\">The Work-Energy Theorem</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The total net mechanical work done by all forces (conservative, non-conservative, internal, and external) acting on a body is identically equal to the net change in its kinetic energy.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Joule (J)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    W_{\text{net}} = K_f - K_i = Delta K = \frac{1}{2} m v_f<sup>2</sup> - \frac{1}{2} m v_i<sup>2</sup>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 15.5px;\">Proof of Work-Energy Theorem for a Variable Force (Calculus Method)</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Differential Work Expression</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By definition, the work done in displacement dx is: dW = F , dx.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Substitute Newton's Second Law</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Substitute F = m a = m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>:\n          <br>dW = m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> , dx = m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dx</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> , dv.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Velocity Identity</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Since instantaneous velocity is v = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dx</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>, we have:\n          <br>dW = m v , dv.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Integration Between Velocity Limits</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Integrate both sides from initial velocity v_i to final velocity v_f:\n          <br>W = int dW = int_{v_i}<sup>v_f</sup> m v , dv = m left[ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> \right]_{v_i}<sup>v_f</sup> = \frac{1}{2} m v_f<sup>2</sup> - \frac{1}{2} m v_i<sup>2</sup>.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">5</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Final Conclusion</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\"><div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold;\">\n          W_{net} = K_f - K_i = Delta K\n        </div>\n        <i>This proves that the work done by a variable force equals the exact change in kinetic energy.</i></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    3. Power & Its Operational Formulations\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    In engineering, it is not merely the total work done that matters, but <b>how rapidly that work is accomplished</b>. A construction crane lifts a metric ton of concrete to the tenth floor of a building in 30 seconds, whereas an individual laborer carrying small buckets takes two full days to perform the exact same mechanical work (mgh). The crane possesses vastly higher <b>power</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #00c6ff; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 16px;\">Power (P)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Power is defined as the time rate of doing mechanical work or the rate at which energy is transferred or transformed.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Watt (W = J s<sup>-1</sup> = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-3</sup>)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    &bull; <b>Average Power (P_{\text{avg}}):</b> Ratio of total work Delta W to total elapsed time Delta t:\n    <br><span style=\"color: #80DEEA; font-weight: bold;\">P_{\text{avg}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">Delta W</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">Delta t</span>\n  </span></span>\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    &bull; <b>Instantaneous Power (P):</b> The limiting value of average power as time interval approaches zero:\n    <br><span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-weight: bold;\">P = lim_{Delta t \to 0} <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">Delta W</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">Delta t</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dW</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span></span>\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    Substituting dW = \u000bec{F} cdot d\u000bec{r}:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #76FF03;\">\n    <b>P = \u000bec{F} cdot <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d\u000bec{r}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{v} = F v cos\theta</b>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Practical Units of Power:</b><br>\n    &bull; <b>Kilowatt (kW):</b> 1\text{ kW} = 10<sup>3</sup>\text{ W}.<br>\n    &bull; <b>Megawatt (MW):</b> 1\text{ MW} = 10<sup>6</sup>\text{ W}.<br>\n    &bull; <b>Horsepower (hp):</b> Imperial unit used for automobile and industrial electric motors: <b>1\text{ hp} = 746\text{ Watts}</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    4. Notion of Potential Energy & Conservative Gradient\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    <b>Potential Energy (U)</b> is the stored energy possessed by a physical system by virtue of its position, spatial configuration, or state of strain within a field of force. Unlike kinetic energy, which belongs to a single moving particle, potential energy is fundamentally a <b>property of a system of two or more interacting bodies</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #00c6ff; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 16px;\">Potential Energy (U)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The potential energy of a body at any position is defined as the negative of the work done by the conservative internal force in displacing the body from a chosen standard reference position (where U = 0) to that position.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Joule (J)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    Delta U = U(x) - U(x_0) = -W_{\text{conservative}} = -int_{x_0}<sup>x</sup> F_{\text{cons}}(x') , dx'\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Fundamental Relation Between Conservative Force and Potential Energy:</b><br>\n    Differentiating both sides of Delta U = -int F dx with respect to position x yields the master differential identity:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #76FF03;\">\n    <b>F(x) = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dU</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span> quad Longleftrightarrow quad \u000bec{F} = -\nabla U = -left( <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">partial U</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">partial x</span>\n  </span>hat{i} + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">partial U</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">partial y</span>\n  </span>hat{j} + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">partial U</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">partial z</span>\n  </span>hat{k} \right)</b>\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    <i>Physical Insight: A conservative force always points in the direction in which potential energy decreases most rapidly (downhill on the potential energy curve).</i>\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Gravitational Potential Energy Near Earth's Surface:</b><br>\n    To raise a body of mass m vertically through height h at constant speed against gravity (\u000bec{F}_g = -mghat{j}):<br>\n    W_{\text{ext}} = int<sub>0</sub><sup>h</sup> mg , dy = mgh.<br>\n    Taking the ground level as standard reference datum (U = 0 at y = 0):\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #80DEEA; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 8px 0;\">\n    U(h) = mgh\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    5. Potential Energy of a Stretched or Compressed Spring\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Consider an ideal, massless helical spring fixed at one end to a rigid wall and attached to a block of mass m resting on a smooth horizontal surface. When the spring is stretched or compressed by displacement x from its natural unstrained equilibrium position (x = 0), it exerts an internal elastic restoring force described by <b>Hooke's Law</b>:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 10px 0;\">\n    F_s = -k x\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    where k is the <b>spring constant (stiffness constant)</b> having SI unit \text{N m}<sup>-1</sup> and dimensions [M<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]. The negative sign indicates that the restoring force always opposes the displacement, pointing back toward the equilibrium center.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 15.5px;\">Derivation of Elastic Potential Energy of a Spring</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">External Force Required</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">To slowly stretch the spring without accelerating the block, the external applied force must balance the restoring force at every instant:\n          <br>F_{ext} = -F_s = -(-kx) = +kx.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Infinitesimal Work</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Work done by the external pulling agent in causing an additional elongation dx is:\n          <br>dW_{ext} = F_{ext} , dx = kx , dx.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Integration Across Elongation Limits</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Total work done in stretching the spring from unstrained state (x = 0) to final elongation x:\n          <br>W_{ext} = int<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> kx' , dx' = k left[ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">x'<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> \right]<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> = \frac{1}{2} k x<sup>2</sup>.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Potential Energy Formula</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By definition, this external work is stored entirely as internal elastic strain potential energy:\n          <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin-top: 6px;\">\n            U_s = \frac{1}{2} k x<sup>2</sup>\n          </div></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Work Done by Spring Force (W_s):</b><br>\n    W_s = int_{x_i}<sup>x_f</sup> (-kx) dx = -\frac{1}{2}k(x_f<sup>2</sup> - x_i<sup>2</sup>) = -(U_f - U_i) = -Delta U_s.<br>\n    Notice that the work done by the spring force depends exclusively on initial and final elongations, proving that <b>the ideal spring force is strictly conservative</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    6. Conservative vs Non-Conservative Forces & Conservation of Energy\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Forces in nature are fundamentally divided into two major thermodynamic classes based on the path-dependence of the work they perform:\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #00c6ff; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 16px;\">Conservative Force</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">A force is said to be conservative if the work done by or against it in moving a particle from one position to another depends exclusively on the initial and final endpoints and is completely independent of the path taken; equivalently, the work done in traversing any closed loop is identically zero.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin: 10px 0;\">\n    oint \u000bec{F}_{\text{cons}} cdot d\u000bec{r} = 0\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    <i>Key Examples of Conservative Forces:</i> Gravitational force, Electrostatic Coulomb force, Ideal spring restoring force, Magnetic force (on static charges).\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #00c6ff; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 16px;\">Non-Conservative Force</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">A force is non-conservative if the work done by it depends explicitly on the specific trajectory or path traversed between two points; over a closed path, net work done is non-zero, and mechanical energy is irreversibly dissipated into heat, sound, or light.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin: 10px 0;\">\n    oint \u000bec{F}_{\text{non-cons}} cdot d\u000bec{r} \ne 0\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    <i>Key Examples of Non-Conservative Forces:</i> Friction, Viscous drag in fluids, Air resistance, Inelastic damping forces.\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- COMPARISON TABLE -->\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); font-size: 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3);\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.15); border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #00c6ff; text-align: left;\">Criterion</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #00c6ff; text-align: left;\">Conservative Force</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #00c6ff; text-align: left;\">Non-Conservative Force</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; font-weight: bold; color: #CBD5E1;\">Path Dependence</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Completely path-independent</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Strictly path-dependent</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; font-weight: bold; color: #CBD5E1;\">Closed Loop Work</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Identically zero (oint \u000bec{F}cdot d\u000bec{r} = 0)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Non-zero (oint \u000bec{F}cdot d\u000bec{r} \ne 0)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; font-weight: bold; color: #CBD5E1;\">Potential Energy</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Associated function exists (F = -dU/dx)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">No potential energy function can be defined</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; font-weight: bold; color: #CBD5E1;\">Mechanical Energy</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Strictly conserved (E = K + U = \text{const})</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Dissipated into thermal/acoustic energy</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Proof of Conservation of Mechanical Energy for a Freely Falling Body:</b><br>\n    Let a body of mass m be dropped from rest from height H above the ground:\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">At Top Point A (height H):</b> Velocity v_A = 0 implies K_A = 0. Potential energy U_A = mgH.\n      <br>Total mechanical energy: E_A = K_A + U_A = 0 + mgH = mathbf{mgH}.\n    </li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">At Intermediate Point B (fallen distance x, height H - x):</b>\n      <br>Using v_B<sup>2</sup> = 0 + 2gx implies K_B = \frac{1}{2}m(2gx) = mgx.\n      <br>Potential energy: U_B = mg(H - x).\n      <br>Total mechanical energy: E_B = mgx + mg(H - x) = mathbf{mgH}.\n    </li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">At Lowest Ground Point C (height 0):</b>\n      <br>Using v_C<sup>2</sup> = 0 + 2gH implies K_C = \frac{1}{2}m(2gH) = mgH.\n      <br>Potential energy: U_C = mg(0) = 0.\n      <br>Total mechanical energy: E_C = mgH + 0 = mathbf{mgH}.\n    </li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>\n    <i>Since E_A = E_B = E_C = mgH, total mechanical energy remains strictly conserved at every point of the descent.</i>\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 7 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    7. Motion in a Vertical Circle\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    When a small body of mass m tied to an inextensible light string of length L is whirled in a vertical circle, the motion is <b>non-uniform circular motion</b> because gravitational pull continuously retards the body during ascent and accelerates it during descent.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 15.5px;\">Dynamics and Critical Velocity in Vertical Circular Motion</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Tension Equation at Angle theta</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Let &theta; be the angle the string makes with the downward vertical.\n          <br>Centripetal force is supplied by the net radial force:\n          <br>T - mg \\cos&theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> &rArr; \\mathbf{T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + mg \\cos&theta;}.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Topmost Point (Crest, theta = 180 degrees)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At the highest point, \\cos 180\\circ = -1:\n          <br>T_{top} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{top}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> - mg.\n          <br>To prevent the string from slacking, tension must be non-negative (T_{top} \\ge 0):\n          <br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{top}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> - mg \\ge 0 &rArr; \\mathbf{v_{top} \\ge &radic;(g L)}.\n          <br><b>Critical Minimum Velocity at Top: v_{top, min} = &radic;(g L).</b></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Lowest Point (Trough, theta = 0 degrees)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By conservation of mechanical energy between lowest point (height 0) and highest point (height 2L):\n          <br>(1 / 2)m v_{bottom}<sup>2</sup> = (1 / 2)m v_{top}<sup>2</sup> + mg(2L).\n          <br>Substitute critical top speed v_{top}<sup>2</sup> = gL:\n          <br>v_{bottom}<sup>2</sup> = gL + 4gL = 5gL.\n          <br><div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin-top: 6px;\">\n            v_{bottom, min} = &radic;(5 g L)\n          </div></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Horizontal Mid-Position (theta = 90 degrees)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At mid-position (height L):\n          <br>(1 / 2)m v_{bottom}<sup>2</sup> = (1 / 2)m v_{mid}<sup>2</sup> + mgL &rArr; 5gL = v_{mid}<sup>2</sup> + 2gL &rArr; \\mathbf{v_{mid} = &radic;(3 g L)}.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">5</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Tension Difference (Bottom vs Top)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At bottom: T_{bottom} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{bottom}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + mg = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m(5gL)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + mg = 6mg.\n          <br>At top: T_{top} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m(gL)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> - mg = 0.\n          <br><div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-top: 6px;\">\n            T_{bottom} - T_{top} = 6 mg\n          </div>\n          <i>The tension difference between trough and crest is always an invariant 6mg, completely independent of the orbital speed!</i></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 8 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    8. Collisions in One & Two Dimensions (Elastic & Inelastic)\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    A <b>collision</b> is an intense mutual interaction between two or more bodies occurring over a relatively short interval of time, during which relatively large internal impulsive forces are exerted, fundamentally altering the velocities and trajectories of the bodies.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #00c6ff; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 16px;\">Coefficient of Restitution (e)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The coefficient of restitution is defined as the ratio of the relative velocity of separation of two colliding bodies after impact to their relative velocity of approach before impact along the line of collision.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Dimensionless & Unitless</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #80DEEA; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    e = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v_2 - v_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">u_1 - u_2</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\text{Velocity of Separation}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\text{Velocity of Approach</span>\n  </span>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Classification of Collisions:</b>\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">1. Perfectly Elastic Collision (e = 1):</b> Both total linear momentum and total kinetic energy are strictly conserved before and after collision. Mechanical deformation is completely restored. <i>Example:</i> Collisions between atomic nuclei and gas molecules.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">2. Inelastic Collision (0 < e < 1):</b> Total linear momentum is conserved, but total kinetic energy is NOT conserved (some kinetic energy is dissipated into heat, sound, or structural deformation). <i>Example:</i> Automobile collisions, dropping a rubber ball onto floor.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #00c6ff;\">3. Completely (Perfectly) Inelastic Collision (e = 0):</b> The two colliding bodies stick together upon impact and move with a single common velocity. Kinetic energy loss is maximal. <i>Example:</i> A bullet embedding inside a wooden pendulum block.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-size: 15.5px;\">Elastic Collision in One Dimension (Head-On Collision)</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Conservation of Linear Momentum</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2 = m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2 &rArr; m_1(u_1 - v_1) = m_2(v_2 - u_2) \\quad \\ldots(1)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Conservation of Kinetic Energy</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">(1 / 2)m_1 u_1<sup>2</sup> + (1 / 2)m_2 u_2<sup>2</sup> = (1 / 2)m_1 v_1<sup>2</sup> + (1 / 2)m_2 v_2<sup>2</sup> &rArr; m_1(u_1<sup>2</sup> - v_1<sup>2</sup>) = m_2(v_2<sup>2</sup> - u_2<sup>2</sup>) \\quad \\ldots(2)</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Relative Velocity Theorem (u1 - u2 = v2 - v1)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Divide equation (2) by equation (1) using difference of squares (a<sup>2</sup> - b<sup>2</sup>) = (a-b)(a+b):\n          <br>u_1 + v_1 = v_2 + u_2 &rArr; \\mathbf{u_1 - u_2 = v_2 - v_1}.\n          <br><i>In any 1D elastic collision, the relative velocity of approach equals the relative velocity of separation (e = 1).</i></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Master Velocity Formulas</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Solving for final velocities v_1 and v_2:\n          <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 8px 0;\">\n            v_1 = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 - m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>) u_1 + (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>) u_2\n          </div>\n          <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 8px 0;\">\n            v_2 = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>) u_1 + (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_2 - m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>) u_2\n          </div></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.2); color: #00c6ff; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #00c6ff;\">5</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #00c6ff; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Special Case: Equal Masses (m1 = m2)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">When m_1 = m_2:\n          <br>v_1 = 0 + u_2 = \\mathbf{u_2} and v_2 = u_1 + 0 = \\mathbf{u_1}.\n          <br><b>Two bodies of identical mass completely swap their velocities upon 1D elastic collision!</b></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Loss of Kinetic Energy in Perfectly Inelastic 1D Collision:</b><br>\n    Two bodies of masses m_1 and m_2 stick together after colliding, moving with common velocity V = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n    The loss of kinetic energy is given by:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #76FF03;\">\n    <b>Delta K = K_i - K_f = \frac{1}{2} left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) (u_1 - u_2)<sup>2</sup></b>\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    <i>Since (u_1 - u_2)<sup>2</sup> > 0, Delta K is strictly positive, proving kinetic energy is always irreversibly lost in completely inelastic collisions.</i>\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Elastic Collision in Two Dimensions (Glancing Collision):</b><br>\n    When colliding bodies do not strike head-on, they scatter in a plane at angles \theta_1 and \theta_2 relative to the incident axis. Vector momentum must be conserved along both x and y axes independently:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #80DEEA; font-size: 15px; margin: 8px 0;\">\n    &bull; x-axis: m_1 u_1 = m_1 v_1 cos\theta_1 + m_2 v_2 cos\theta_2<br>\n    &bull; y-axis: 0 = m_1 v_1 sin\theta_1 - m_2 v_2 sin\theta_2\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    <b>Right-Angle Scattering Property:</b> When a particle of mass m strikes an identical stationary particle (m_1 = m_2, u_2 = 0) elastically in 2D, the two particles strictly scatter at right angles to one another: <b>\theta_1 + \theta_2 = 90<sup>c</sup>irc</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- MASTER REVISION FORMULA CHEAT SHEET -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      ⚡ Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet\n    </h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Comprehensive Operational Equations &bull; Unit IV: Work, Energy and Power (06 Marks)\n    </p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Work Formulas:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Constant Force: W = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{s} = F s cos\theta<br>\n          Variable Force: W = int_{x_1}<sup>x_2</sup> F(x) , dx<br>\n          Area under F-x curve = \text{Work Done}<br>\n          1\text{ J} = 10<sup>7</sup>\text{ erg} = 1\text{ N m}\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Kinetic Energy & Theorem:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          K = \frac{1}{2} m v<sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">p<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2m</span>\n  </span><br>\n          p = sqrt{2 m K}<br>\n          Work-Energy: W_{\text{net}} = K_f - K_i = Delta K<br>\n          P = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dW</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{v} quad (1\text{ hp} = 746\text{ W})\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Potential Energy & Springs:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Gravitational: U = mgh<br>\n          Conservative: F(x) = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dU</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span><br>\n          Hooke's Law: F_s = -kx<br>\n          Spring Potential: U_s = \frac{1}{2} k x<sup>2</sup>\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Vertical Circular Motion:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Tension: T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + mg cos\theta<br>\n          Top Speed (Min): v_{\text{top}} = sqrt{g L}<br>\n          Bottom Speed (Min): v_{\text{bottom}} = sqrt{5 g L}<br>\n          Mid Speed (Min): v_{\text{mid}} = sqrt{3 g L}<br>\n          T_{\text{bottom}} - T_{\text{top}} = 6 mg\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; 1D Elastic Collisions:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          u_1 - u_2 = v_2 - v_1 quad (e = 1)<br>\n          v_1 = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 - m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right)u_1 + left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right)u_2<br>\n          v_2 = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right)u_1 + left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_2 - m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right)u_2<br>\n          m_1 = m_2 implies v_1 = u_2, v_2 = u_1\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Inelastic & 2D Collisions:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Common Velocity: V = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span><br>\n          Kinetic Energy Loss: Delta K = \frac{1}{2}left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right)(u_1 - u_2)<sup>2</sup><br>\n          2D Glancing (m_1 = m_2, u_2 = 0): \theta_1 + \theta_2 = 90<sup>c</sup>irc$\n        </span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>";

export const c11Phy4HtmlSolutions = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- TITLE BANNER -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #00c6ff; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 22px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      NCERT & Master Solutions Guide (100 Questions)\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Class 11 Physics Unit IV: Work, Energy and Power (06 Marks) &bull; Official Syllabus Coverage\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION A: VSA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark each) &bull; Q1 to Q40\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Fundamental definitions, 1-line physical reasoning, SI units, dimensions, and foundational conservation laws.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q1: Define work done by a constant force in classical mechanics. Is it a scalar or vector quantity?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Work done by a constant force is defined as the scalar (dot) product of the applied force vector and displacement vector: <b>W = F &bull; s = F s cos &theta;</b>.<br>Work is a <b>scalar quantity</b> possessing magnitude and algebraic sign, but no spatial direction.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q2: State the SI unit and dimensional formula of work and energy.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>SI Unit:</b> Joule (J = N m = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>) &bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q3: Define the absolute SI unit of work: One Joule (1 J).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>One Joule (1 J)</b> of work is defined as the mechanical work performed when a constant force of 1 Newton displaces a body by a distance of 1 meter in the direction of the force.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q4: Define 1 erg (CGS unit of work) and state its mathematical relationship with 1 Joule.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>1 erg</b> is the work done when a force of 1 dyne displaces an object through 1 cm in the direction of the force.<br>1 J = 1 N &times; 1 m = (10<sup>5</sup> dyne) &times; (10<sup>2</sup> cm) = <b>10<sup>7</sup> erg</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q5: Under what condition is the work done by a force classified as positive? Give an example.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Work done is positive when the angle &theta; between force and displacement is acute (<b>0&deg; &le; &theta; < 90&deg;</b>, so cos &theta; > 0).<br><i>Example:</i> Work done by gravity on a freely falling body (&theta; = 0&deg;, W = +mgh).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q6: Under what condition is the work done by a force classified as negative? Give an example.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Work done is negative when the angle &theta; between force and displacement is obtuse (<b>90&deg; < &theta; &le; 180&deg;</b>, so cos &theta; < 0).<br><i>Example:</i> Work done by kinetic friction on a sliding block (&theta; = 180&deg;, W = -f s).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q7: State three distinct physical conditions under which the work done by a force is identically zero.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      1. <b>Zero Displacement:</b> s = 0 (e.g. pushing a stationary wall).<br>2. <b>Zero Force:</b> F = 0 (e.g. motion in deep space).<br>3. <b>Orthogonal Direction:</b> &theta; = 90&deg; &rArr; cos 90&deg; = 0 (e.g. centripetal force in circular motion).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q8: Why is the work done by a porter carrying a suitcase horizontally on his head considered zero against gravity?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The downward gravitational force (mg) acting on the luggage is directed vertically downward, while the porter moves horizontally along the platform (&theta; = 90&deg;). Since cos 90&deg; = 0, work done by gravity is strictly <b>zero</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q9: Does the Earth perform any work on a satellite moving in a circular orbit around it? Explain.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>No.</b> In a circular orbit, the gravitational pull of the Earth acts radially inward toward the center, while the instantaneous displacement is tangential along the orbital path (&theta; = 90&deg;). W = F s cos 90&deg; = <b>0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q10: Write the mathematical formula for work done by a variable force F(x) along the x-axis from x_1 to x_2.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The work done by a position-dependent force F(x) is given by the definite integral: <b>W = &int;<sub>x<sub>1</sub></sub><sup>x<sub>2</sub></sup> F(x) dx</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q11: What physical quantity is represented by the geometric area under a Force-Displacement (F-x) graph?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The geometric area under the Force-Displacement (F-x) curve bounded by the displacement axis represents the <b>total mechanical work done (W)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q12: Define Kinetic Energy of a body. State its mathematical formula.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Kinetic Energy is the energy possessed by a body by virtue of its translational motion, measured by the total work done in accelerating it from rest to speed v: <b>K = &frac12; m v<sup>2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q13: State the mathematical relation connecting kinetic energy K, linear momentum p, and mass m.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>K = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">p<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2 m</span>\n  </span></b> &bull; Inversely: <b>p = &radic;(2 m K)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q14: Can the kinetic energy of an object ever be negative? Justify.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>No.</b> Mass m is always positive, and the square of velocity (v<sup>2</sup> = v &bull; v) is mathematically non-negative (v<sup>2</sup> &ge; 0). Hence K = &frac12; m v<sup>2</sup> is strictly <b>zero or positive</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q15: If the linear momentum of a moving body is doubled, by what factor does its kinetic energy increase?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Since K = p<sup>2</sup> / (2m), if p' = 2p, then K' = (2p)<sup>2</sup> / (2m) = 4 [p<sup>2</sup> / (2m)] = <b>4 K</b>.<br>The kinetic energy increases by a factor of <b>4 (400%)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q16: State the Work-Energy Theorem.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The total net work done by all forces (conservative, non-conservative, internal, and external) acting on an object is identically equal to the net change in its kinetic energy: <b>W<sub>net</sub> = &Delta;K = K<sub>f</sub> - K<sub>i</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q17: Does the Work-Energy Theorem hold true for non-uniform variable forces?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>Yes.</b> The Work-Energy Theorem is a universal mathematical theorem derived directly from Newton's Second Law and holds unconditionally for both constant and variable forces.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q18: Define Power. State its SI base unit and dimensional formula.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Power is defined as the time rate of doing mechanical work: <b>P = dW/dt</b>.<br><b>SI Unit:</b> Watt (W = J s<sup>-1</sup> = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-3</sup>) &bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q19: Define the SI unit of power: One Watt (1 W).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>One Watt (1 W)</b> is defined as the power of an engine or agent that performs mechanical work at the rate of exactly 1 Joule per second (1 W = 1 J s<sup>-1</sup>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q20: Define Horsepower (hp) and state its numerical equivalent in Watts.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>Horsepower (hp)</b> is an imperial engineering unit of power.<br><b>1 hp = 746 Watts (0.746 kW)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q21: Express instantaneous power P in terms of force vector F and velocity vector v.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Since dW = F &bull; dr, instantaneous power is the dot product: <b>P = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dW</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = F &bull; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dr</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = F &bull; v = F v cos &theta;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q22: Define the commercial unit of electrical energy: One Kilowatt-hour (1 kWh), and express it in Joules.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>1 kWh</b> is the total electrical energy consumed by a device of power 1 kilowatt operating continuously for 1 hour.<br>1 kWh = (1000 W) &times; (3600 s) = <b>3.6 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> Joules (3.6 MJ)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q23: Define Potential Energy. Can potential energy be associated with an isolated single particle in empty space?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Potential energy is the stored energy possessed by a body by virtue of its position, configuration, or spatial strain in a force field.<br><b>No.</b> Potential energy is fundamentally a mutual interaction property of a <b>system of two or more particles</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q24: Write the formula for gravitational potential energy of a mass m at height h above the Earth's surface (h << R_E).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Taking ground level as zero reference datum: <b>U(h) = m g h</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q25: State the fundamental calculus relation connecting a one-dimensional conservative force F(x) and its potential energy function U(x).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The conservative force equals the negative spatial derivative (negative gradient) of potential energy: <b>F(x) = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dU</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q26: State Hooke's Law for an ideal helical spring.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Within the elastic limit, the restoring force developed in a spring is directly proportional to its displacement from equilibrium and directed opposite to it: <b>F<sub>s</sub> = -k x</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q27: Define the spring constant (force constant) k. State its SI unit and dimensions.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Spring constant k is the restoring force produced per unit displacement: k = |F<sub>s</sub>| / x.<br><b>SI Unit:</b> N m<sup>-1</sup> &bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q28: Write the formula for the elastic potential energy stored in a spring stretched or compressed by displacement x.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>U<sub>s</sub> = &frac12; k x<sup>2</sup></b>, where k is the spring constant.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q29: Define a Conservative Force. State two examples.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      A force is conservative if the work done by it on a particle depends solely on initial and final positions and is independent of the path taken (&oint; F &bull; dr = 0).<br><i>Examples:</i> <b>Gravitational force</b>, <b>Electrostatic Coulomb force</b>, and <b>Ideal spring force</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q30: Define a Non-Conservative Force. State two examples.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      A force is non-conservative if the work done by it depends explicitly on the path traversed, irreversibly dissipating mechanical energy as heat.<br><i>Examples:</i> <b>Frictional force</b> and <b>Viscous drag</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q31: What is the net work done by a conservative force in moving a particle around any closed loop?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The net work done by a conservative force around any closed path is <b>identically zero (&oint; F<sub>cons</sub> &bull; dr = 0)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q32: State the Principle of Conservation of Mechanical Energy.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The total mechanical energy (sum of kinetic and potential energy: E = K + U) of an isolated system remains strictly constant over time if the internal forces doing work are purely conservative: <b>&Delta;K + &Delta;U = 0 &rArr; E = constant</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q33: What is the minimum critical velocity at the topmost point of a vertical circular loop of radius L to prevent the string from slackening?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      To ensure non-zero tension at the crest (T<sub>top</sub> &ge; 0), the minimum critical velocity is: <b>v<sub>top</sub> = &radic;(g L)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q34: What is the minimum speed required at the lowest point of a vertical circle of radius L for a body to loop the full circle?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      By conservation of mechanical energy, the minimum speed at the trough must be: <b>v<sub>bottom</sub> = &radic;(5 g L)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q35: What is the difference in string tension between the lowest and highest points in vertical circular motion?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The difference in string tension between the trough and crest is strictly an invariant constant: <b>T<sub>bottom</sub> - T<sub>top</sub> = 6 m g</b> (independent of speed!).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q36: Define the Coefficient of Restitution (e) in collision physics.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The coefficient of restitution is defined as the ratio of relative velocity of separation to relative velocity of approach along the line of impact: <b>e = (v<sub>2</sub> - v<sub>1</sub>) / (u<sub>1</sub> - u<sub>2</sub>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q37: State the numerical value of the coefficient of restitution e for: (a) Perfectly elastic collision, (b) Perfectly inelastic collision.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>(a) Perfectly Elastic Collision:</b> e = <b>1</b>.<br><b>(b) Perfectly Inelastic Collision:</b> e = <b>0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q38: What physical quantities are strictly conserved in an Elastic Collision?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      In an elastic collision, both <b>Total Linear Momentum</b> and <b>Total Kinetic Energy</b> are strictly conserved.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q39: What physical quantities are conserved in an Inelastic Collision?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      In an inelastic collision, <b>Total Linear Momentum</b> is strictly conserved, but <b>Kinetic Energy is NOT conserved</b> (a fraction is converted into thermal or deformation energy).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q40: What occurs when two bodies of identical mass m undergo a head-on elastic collision in one dimension?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      When two identical masses collide elastically in 1D, they <b>completely exchange their velocities</b> (v<sub>1</sub> = u<sub>2</sub> and v<sub>2</sub> = u<sub>1</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION B: SA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 & 3 Marks each) &bull; Q41 to Q80\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Core board numericals, variable force integrals, power calculations, spring energy, vertical circles, and collision analysis.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q41: A force F = (3 î + 4 ĵ - 5 k̂) N acts on a particle and produces a displacement s = (5 î + 4 ĵ + 3 k̂) m. Calculate: (a) Work done by the force, (b) Angle between force and displacement vectors.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>(a) Work Done:</b> W = F &bull; s = (3)(5) + (4)(4) + (-5)(3) = 15 + 16 - 15 = <b>16 Joules</b>.<br><b>(b) Angle &theta;:</b><br>|F| = &radic;[3<sup>2</sup> + 4<sup>2</sup> + (-5)<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[9 + 16 + 25] = &radic;50 = 5&radic;2 N.<br>|s| = &radic;[5<sup>2</sup> + 4<sup>2</sup> + 3<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[25 + 16 + 9] = &radic;50 = 5&radic;2 m.<br>cos &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">W</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">|F| |s|</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">16</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">(5&radic;2)(5&radic;2)</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">16</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">50</span>\n  </span> = 0.32.<br><b>&theta; = cos<sup>-1</sup>(0.32) &approx; 71.34&deg;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q42: A position-dependent variable force F(x) = (3 x² + 2 x - 7) N acts on a 2 kg body moving along the x-axis. Calculate the work done in displacing the body from x = 0 to x = 5 meters.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      W = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>5</sup> F(x) dx = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>5</sup> (3 x<sup>2</sup> + 2 x - 7) dx = [x<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> - 7x]<sub>0</sub><sup>5</sup>.<br>At x = 5: W = (5)<sup>3</sup> + (5)<sup>2</sup> - 7(5) = 125 + 25 - 35 = <b>115 Joules</b>.<br>At x = 0: W = 0.<br><b>Total Work Done = 115 Joules</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q43: A ball of mass 0.5 kg slows down from an initial speed of 20 m/s to 10 m/s due to air resistance. Calculate the total work done by the resistive force using the Work-Energy Theorem.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      m = 0.5 kg, v<sub>i</sub> = 20 m/s, v<sub>f</sub> = 10 m/s.<br>Initial Kinetic Energy: K<sub>i</sub> = &frac12; m v<sub>i</sub><sup>2</sup> = &frac12;(0.5)(20)<sup>2</sup> = 0.25 &times; 400 = <b>100 J</b>.<br>Final Kinetic Energy: K<sub>f</sub> = &frac12; m v<sub>f</sub><sup>2</sup> = &frac12;(0.5)(10)<sup>2</sup> = 0.25 &times; 100 = <b>25 J</b>.<br>By Work-Energy Theorem: W = &Delta;K = K<sub>f</sub> - K<sub>i</sub> = 25 - 100 = <b>-75 Joules</b>.<br>The negative sign indicates work was done against motion by resistive drag.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q44: A bullet of mass 20 grams moving horizontally at 250 m/s strikes a wooden target and penetrates a depth of 12.5 cm before halting. Calculate the average retarding force exerted by the wood.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      m = 20 g = 0.02 kg, u = 250 m/s, v = 0, s = 12.5 cm = 0.125 m.<br>Initial Kinetic Energy: K<sub>i</sub> = &frac12; m u<sup>2</sup> = &frac12;(0.02)(250)<sup>2</sup> = 0.01 &times; 62500 = <b>625 Joules</b>.<br>Final Kinetic Energy: K<sub>f</sub> = 0.<br>By Work-Energy Theorem: W = -f s = &Delta;K = -625 J.<br>f &times; 0.125 = 625 &rArr; f = 625 / 0.125 = <b>5000 Newtons (5 kN)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q45: An electric water pump lifts 3600 kg of water per minute from an underground borehole 20 meters deep. Calculate the power developed by the pump in kW and Horsepower (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Mass m = 3600 kg, h = 20 m, t = 1 minute = 60 seconds, g = 9.8 m/s².<br>Work done: W = m g h = 3600 &times; 9.8 &times; 20 = <b>705,600 Joules</b>.<br>Power in Watts: P = W / t = 705600 / 60 = <b>11,760 Watts = 11.76 kW</b>.<br>Power in Horsepower: P = 11760 / 746 &approx; <b>15.76 hp</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q46: A car of mass 1000 kg accelerates uniformly from rest to a speed of 72 km/h in 10 seconds along a horizontal road. Calculate: (a) Average power, (b) Instantaneous power at t = 10 s.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      m = 1000 kg, u = 0, v = 72 km/h = 20 m/s, t = 10 s.<br>Acceleration: a = (20 - 0) / 10 = <b>2 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.<br>Force: F = m a = 1000 &times; 2 = <b>2000 N</b>.<br><b>(a) Average Power:</b> Total work W = &Delta;K = &frac12;(1000)(20)<sup>2</sup> = 200,000 J.<br>P<sub>avg</sub> = W / t = 200000 / 10 = <b>20,000 W = 20 kW</b>.<br><b>(b) Instantaneous Power at t = 10 s:</b> P = F &times; v = 2000 N &times; 20 m/s = <b>40,000 W = 40 kW</b>.<br>(Notice that P<sub>inst</sub> at final speed is exactly twice P<sub>avg</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q47: State and prove the Work-Energy Theorem for a constant force acting in one dimension.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>Statement:</b> The net work done by an applied force on a body equals the change in its kinetic energy.<br><b>Proof:</b> Let a constant force F act on mass m, changing velocity from u to v over displacement s.<br>By definition: W = F &times; s = (m a) s.<br>From kinematics: v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2 a s &rArr; a s = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span>.<br>Substituting into work formula:<br>W = m(a s) = m [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span>] = &frac12; m v<sup>2</sup> - &frac12; m u<sup>2</sup> = <b>K<sub>f</sub> - K<sub>i</sub> = &Delta;K</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q48: If the kinetic energy of an object is increased by 300%, calculate the percentage increase in its linear momentum.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Let initial kinetic energy be K<sub>1</sub> and initial momentum be p<sub>1</sub> = &radic;(2m K<sub>1</sub>).<br>New kinetic energy: K<sub>2</sub> = K<sub>1</sub> + 300% of K<sub>1</sub> = K<sub>1</sub> + 3K<sub>1</sub> = <b>4 K<sub>1</sub></b>.<br>New momentum: p<sub>2</sub> = &radic;(2m K<sub>2</sub>) = &radic;(2m &times; 4K<sub>1</sub>) = 2 &radic;(2m K<sub>1</sub>) = <b>2 p<sub>1</sub></b>.<br>Fractional increase: (p<sub>2</sub> - p<sub>1</sub>) / p<sub>1</sub> = (2p<sub>1</sub> - p<sub>1</sub>) / p<sub>1</sub> = 1.<br><b>Percentage Increase = 100%</b> (momentum doubles).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q49: If the linear momentum of a moving body is increased by 50%, calculate the percentage increase in its kinetic energy.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Let initial momentum be p<sub>1</sub>, so K<sub>1</sub> = p<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> / (2m).<br>New momentum: p<sub>2</sub> = p<sub>1</sub> + 0.5 p<sub>1</sub> = <b>1.5 p<sub>1</sub></b>.<br>New kinetic energy: K<sub>2</sub> = p<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> / (2m) = (1.5 p<sub>1</sub>)<sup>2</sup> / (2m) = 2.25 [p<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> / (2m)] = <b>2.25 K<sub>1</sub></b>.<br>Percentage increase = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">K<sub>2</sub> - K<sub>1</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">K<sub>1</sub></span>\n  </span> &times; 100% = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2.25 K<sub>1</sub> - K<sub>1</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">K<sub>1</sub></span>\n  </span> &times; 100% = 1.25 &times; 100% = <b>125%</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q50: Derive the fundamental relation connecting a one-dimensional conservative force F(x) and its potential energy function U(x): F(x) = -dU/dx.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      By definition, the change in potential energy &Delta;U in an infinitesimal displacement dx equals the negative of work done by the conservative force:<br>dU = -dW<sub>cons</sub> = -[F(x) dx].<br>Dividing both sides by dx:<br><b>F(x) = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dU</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span></b>.<br><b>Physical Significance:</b> The negative sign confirms that conservative forces always act in the direction of decreasing potential energy (spontaneous tendency toward lowest energy state).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q51: The potential energy of a particle moving along the x-axis is given by U(x) = (2 x⁴ - 5 x² + 6) Joules. Find the force acting on the particle at x = 2 meters.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Using F(x) = -dU/dx:<br>dU/dx = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span>(2x<sup>4</sup> - 5x<sup>2</sup> + 6) = 8x<sup>3</sup> - 10x.<br>F(x) = -(8x<sup>3</sup> - 10x) = -8x<sup>3</sup> + 10x.<br>At x = 2 m:<br>F(2) = -8(2)<sup>3</sup> + 10(2) = -8(8) + 20 = -64 + 20 = <b>-44 Newtons</b>.<br>The negative sign indicates the force acts in the negative x-direction.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q52: A spring has a spring constant k = 500 N/m. Calculate the work required to stretch the spring from an elongation of 2 cm to 6 cm from its unstrained length.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Given: k = 500 N/m, x<sub>1</sub> = 2 cm = 0.02 m, x<sub>2</sub> = 6 cm = 0.06 m.<br>Work done: W = &frac12; k (x<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> - x<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>) = &frac12;(500) [(0.06)<sup>2</sup> - (0.02)<sup>2</sup>]<br>W = 250 [0.0036 - 0.0004] = 250 &times; 0.0032 = <b>0.80 Joules</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q53: Prove analytically that the elastic restoring force of an ideal spring is a Conservative Force.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Let a spring be stretched from x = 0 to elongation +x and then allowed to return back to x = 0.<br>&bull; Work during expansion (0 to x): W<sub>1</sub> = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> (-kx') dx' = -&frac12; k x<sup>2</sup>.<br>&bull; Work during contraction (x to 0): W<sub>2</sub> = &int;<sub>x</sub><sup>0</sup> (-kx') dx' = [-&frac12; k x'<sup>2</sup>]<sub>x</sub><sup>0</sup> = 0 - (-&frac12; k x<sup>2</sup>) = +&frac12; k x<sup>2</sup>.<br>Total work done in the closed round trip:<br>W<sub>net</sub> = W<sub>1</sub> + W<sub>2</sub> = -&frac12; k x<sup>2</sup> + &frac12; k x<sup>2</sup> = <b>0</b>.<br>Since &oint; F<sub>s</sub> &bull; dr = 0, the spring force is strictly <b>conservative</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q54: A body of mass 2 kg attached to a horizontal spring of stiffness k = 800 N/m is pulled to a displacement of 10 cm and released from rest on a frictionless floor. Calculate the maximum speed attained by the body.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      m = 2 kg, k = 800 N/m, amplitude A = 10 cm = 0.10 m.<br>By conservation of mechanical energy, potential energy at maximum stretch converts entirely to kinetic energy at equilibrium position (x = 0):<br>&frac12; m v<sub>max</sub><sup>2</sup> = &frac12; k A<sup>2</sup> &rArr; v<sub>max</sub> = A &radic;(k / m).<br>v<sub>max</sub> = 0.10 &times; &radic;(800 / 2) = 0.10 &times; &radic;400 = 0.10 &times; 20 = <b>2.0 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q55: Construct an analytical comparison between Conservative Forces and Non-Conservative Forces across four key physical criteria.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      1. <b>Path Dependence:</b> Conservative work is path-independent; non-conservative work depends on specific path.<br>2. <b>Closed Loop Work:</b> Conservative &oint; F &bull; dr = 0; non-conservative &oint; F &bull; dr &ne; 0.<br>3. <b>Potential Energy:</b> Conservative forces have an associated potential energy (F = -dU/dx); non-conservative forces have no potential energy function.<br>4. <b>Mechanical Energy:</b> Conservative forces conserve total mechanical energy (E = K + U = const); non-conservative forces dissipate energy into heat/sound.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q56: State the Principle of Conservation of Mechanical Energy. Prove it for a freely falling stone of mass m dropped from height H at three points: top (A), midpoint (B), and ground impact (C).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>Statement:</b> Total mechanical energy E = K + U remains constant when only conservative forces act.<br><b>Proof:</b><br>&bull; <b>Point A (Height H):</b> v = 0 &rArr; K<sub>A</sub> = 0, U<sub>A</sub> = mgH &rArr; <b>E<sub>A</sub> = mgH</b>.<br>&bull; <b>Point B (Fallen x, Height H - x):</b> v<sub>B</sub><sup>2</sup> = 2gx &rArr; K<sub>B</sub> = &frac12;m(2gx) = mgx. U<sub>B</sub> = mg(H - x).<br>E<sub>B</sub> = mgx + mg(H - x) = <b>mgH</b>.<br>&bull; <b>Point C (Ground, Height 0):</b> v<sub>C</sub><sup>2</sup> = 2gH &rArr; K<sub>C</sub> = &frac12;m(2gH) = mgH. U<sub>C</sub> = 0.<br>E<sub>C</sub> = mgH + 0 = <b>mgH</b>.<br><b>Since E<sub>A</sub> = E<sub>B</sub> = E<sub>C</sub> = mgH, mechanical energy is strictly conserved.</b>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q57: A small object of mass m tied to an inextensible string of length L is whirled in a vertical circle. Derive the expression for string tension T at any general position inclined at angle &theta; with the downward vertical.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      At angle &theta; with downward vertical, the radial forces acting on mass m are:<br>&bull; Inward tension T along string toward center.<br>&bull; Outward radial component of gravity: mg cos &theta;.<br>The net inward force supplies the centripetal acceleration:<br>T - mg cos &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span>.<br><b>T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + m g cos &theta;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q58: A stone of mass 0.2 kg tied to a string 0.5 m long is whirled in a vertical circle. Calculate: (a) Minimum critical velocity at the top, (b) Minimum critical velocity at the bottom, (c) String tension at the bottom (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      m = 0.2 kg, L = 0.5 m, g = 9.8 m/s².<br><b>(a) Top Velocity:</b> v<sub>top</sub> = &radic;(g L) = &radic;(9.8 &times; 0.5) = &radic;4.9 &approx; <b>2.21 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br><b>(b) Bottom Velocity:</b> v<sub>bottom</sub> = &radic;(5 g L) = &radic;(5 &times; 9.8 &times; 0.5) = &radic;24.5 &approx; <b>4.95 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br><b>(c) Tension at Bottom:</b> T<sub>bottom</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sub>bottom</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + mg = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">0.2 &times; 24.5</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">0.5</span>\n  </span> + 0.2(9.8) = 9.8 + 1.96 = <b>11.76 Newtons (= 6 mg)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q59: Prove analytically that in vertical circular motion of a bob tied to a string, the difference in string tension between the lowest and highest points is an invariant constant equal to 6 mg.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Tension at bottom (&theta; = 0&deg;): T<sub>b</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sub>b</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + mg.<br>Tension at top (&theta; = 180&deg;): T<sub>t</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sub>t</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> - mg.<br>Difference: T<sub>b</sub> - T<sub>t</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span>(v<sub>b</sub><sup>2</sup> - v<sub>t</sub><sup>2</sup>) + 2mg.<br>By conservation of energy between bottom and top (height difference = 2L):<br>&frac12; m v<sub>b</sub><sup>2</sup> = &frac12; m v<sub>t</sub><sup>2</sup> + mg(2L) &rArr; v<sub>b</sub><sup>2</sup> - v<sub>t</sub><sup>2</sup> = 4 g L.<br>Substitute (v<sub>b</sub><sup>2</sup> - v<sub>t</sub><sup>2</sup>):<br>T<sub>b</sub> - T<sub>t</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span>(4 g L) + 2 mg = 4 mg + 2 mg = <b>6 mg</b>.<br>This confirms the tension difference is strictly <b>6 mg</b> regardless of launch speed.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q60: A body executes vertical circular motion with velocity v_b = &radic;(7 g L) at its lowest point. Calculate its velocity and string tension at the highest point in terms of m, g, and L.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Given: v<sub>b</sub><sup>2</sup> = 7 g L.<br><b>(a) Top Velocity:</b> Using v<sub>t</sub><sup>2</sup> = v<sub>b</sub><sup>2</sup> - 4 g L = 7 g L - 4 g L = 3 g L &rArr; <b>v<sub>t</sub> = &radic;(3 g L)</b>.<br><b>(b) Tension at Top:</b> T<sub>t</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sub>t</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> - mg = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m(3 g L)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> - mg = 3 mg - mg = <b>2 mg</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q61: Classify physical collisions into three distinct types based on kinetic energy conservation and the coefficient of restitution (e).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      1. <b>Perfectly Elastic Collision (e = 1):</b> Momentum and kinetic energy both conserved; zero permanent deformation.<br>2. <b>Inelastic Collision (0 < e < 1):</b> Momentum conserved; kinetic energy partially lost to heat/sound/deformation.<br>3. <b>Perfectly Inelastic Collision (e = 0):</b> Colliding bodies stick together and move with single common velocity; kinetic energy loss is maximal.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q62: Define the Coefficient of Restitution (e). Prove that e = 1 for a one-dimensional elastic collision.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>Definition:</b> e = (v<sub>2</sub> - v<sub>1</sub>) / (u<sub>1</sub> - u<sub>2</sub>).<br><b>Proof for 1D Elastic Collision:</b><br>From momentum conservation: m<sub>1</sub>(u<sub>1</sub> - v<sub>1</sub>) = m<sub>2</sub>(v<sub>2</sub> - u<sub>2</sub>) &nbsp;&hellip;(1)<br>From kinetic energy conservation: m<sub>1</sub>(u<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> - v<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>) = m<sub>2</sub>(v<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> - u<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>) &nbsp;&hellip;(2)<br>Dividing (2) by (1) gives: u<sub>1</sub> + v<sub>1</sub> = v<sub>2</sub> + u<sub>2</sub> &rArr; <b>v<sub>2</sub> - v<sub>1</sub> = u<sub>1</sub> - u<sub>2</sub></b>.<br>Therefore: e = (v<sub>2</sub> - v<sub>1</sub>) / (u<sub>1</sub> - u<sub>2</sub>) = <b>1</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q63: A ball of mass 2 kg moving at 6 m/s collides head-on elastically with a stationary ball of mass 4 kg. Calculate the final velocities of both balls.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      m<sub>1</sub> = 2 kg, u<sub>1</sub> = 6 m/s; m<sub>2</sub> = 4 kg, u<sub>2</sub> = 0.<br>Using 1D elastic collision formulas:<br>v<sub>1</sub> = [ (m<sub>1</sub> - m<sub>2</sub>) / (m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>) ] u<sub>1</sub> = [ (2 - 4) / (2 + 4) ] &times; 6 = (-2 / 6) &times; 6 = <b>-2 m s<sup>-1</sup></b> (rebounds backward).<br>v<sub>2</sub> = [ 2 m<sub>1</sub> / (m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>) ] u<sub>1</sub> = [ 2(2) / 6 ] &times; 6 = <b>+4 m s<sup>-1</sup></b> (moves forward).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q64: Two identical railway wagons of mass 10,000 kg each collide. Wagon A moves at 4 m/s and collides with stationary Wagon B. They couple together upon impact. Calculate: (a) Common velocity, (b) Loss in kinetic energy.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      m<sub>1</sub> = m<sub>2</sub> = 10,000 kg, u<sub>1</sub> = 4 m/s, u<sub>2</sub> = 0.<br><b>(a) Common Velocity:</b> V = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m<sub>1</sub> u<sub>1</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub></span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">10000 &times; 4</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">20000</span>\n  </span> = <b>2 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br><b>(b) Kinetic Energy Loss:</b><br>&Delta;K = &frac12; [ (m<sub>1</sub> m<sub>2</sub>) / (m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>) ] (u<sub>1</sub> - u<sub>2</sub>)<sup>2</sup> = &frac12; [ (10000 &times; 10000) / 20000 ] (4)<sup>2</sup><br>&Delta;K = &frac12;(5000)(16) = <b>40,000 Joules (40 kJ)</b> (50% of initial kinetic energy is lost).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q65: A bullet of mass 10 grams moving horizontally at 400 m/s embeds into a wooden block of mass 3.99 kg suspended by cords (Ballistic Pendulum). Calculate the vertical height h to which the block rises (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      m = 10 g = 0.01 kg, M = 3.99 kg, u = 400 m/s.<br>Total combined mass = m + M = 4.0 kg.<br>By momentum conservation: (m + M) V = m u &rArr; 4.0 V = 0.01 &times; 400 = 4 &rArr; <b>V = 1.0 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br>By energy conservation: &frac12; (m + M) V<sup>2</sup> = (m + M) g h &rArr; h = V<sup>2</sup> / (2g).<br>h = (1.0)<sup>2</sup> / (2 &times; 9.8) = 1 / 19.6 &approx; <b>0.051 m = 5.1 cm</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q66: In a 1D elastic collision between projectile m_1 and stationary target m_2, analyze the final velocities when: (a) Projectile is vastly heavier (m_1 >> m_2), (b) Projectile is vastly lighter (m_1 << m_2).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Target stationary (u<sub>2</sub> = 0).<br><b>(a) Massive Projectile (m<sub>1</sub> &gg; m<sub>2</sub>):</b><br>v<sub>1</sub> &approx; (m<sub>1</sub>/m<sub>1</sub>) u<sub>1</sub> = <b>u<sub>1</sub></b> (continues almost unaffected).<br>v<sub>2</sub> &approx; (2m<sub>1</sub>/m<sub>1</sub>) u<sub>1</sub> = <b>2 u<sub>1</sub></b> (light target flies off with double projectile speed!).<br><b>(b) Very Light Projectile (m<sub>1</sub> &ll; m<sub>2</sub>):</b><br>v<sub>1</sub> &approx; (-m<sub>2</sub>/m<sub>2</sub>) u<sub>1</sub> = <b>-u<sub>1</sub></b> (light projectile rebounds with same speed).<br>v<sub>2</sub> &approx; (2m<sub>1</sub>/m<sub>2</sub>) u<sub>1</sub> &approx; <b>0</b> (massive target remains stationary).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q67: A rubber ball is dropped from an initial height h_0 = 10 m onto a rigid horizontal floor. If the coefficient of restitution is e = 0.8, calculate the height h_1 to which the ball rebounds after the first bounce.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Impact velocity: v = &radic;(2 g h<sub>0</sub>).<br>Rebound velocity: v' = e v = e &radic;(2 g h<sub>0</sub>).<br>Rebound height: h<sub>1</sub> = (v')<sup>2</sup> / (2g) = [e<sup>2</sup> (2 g h<sub>0</sub>)] / (2g) = <b>e<sup>2</sup> h<sub>0</sub></b>.<br>h<sub>1</sub> = (0.8)<sup>2</sup> &times; 10 = 0.64 &times; 10 = <b>6.4 meters</b>.<br>(General formula after n bounces: h<sub>n</sub> = e<sup>2n</sup> h<sub>0</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q68: Two particles of identical mass m collide elastically in two dimensions with one initially at rest. Prove that they scatter at strictly 90° to each other (orthogonal scattering).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Let m<sub>1</sub> = m<sub>2</sub> = m, and target initially at rest (u<sub>2</sub> = 0).<br>&bull; Momentum conservation: m u<sub>1</sub> = m v<sub>1</sub> + m v<sub>2</sub> &rArr; <b>u<sub>1</sub> = v<sub>1</sub> + v<sub>2</sub></b>.<br>Squaring both sides (dot product): u<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> = v<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> + v<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> + 2 (v<sub>1</sub> &bull; v<sub>2</sub>) &nbsp;&hellip;(1)<br>&bull; Kinetic energy conservation: &frac12; m u<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> = &frac12; m v<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> + &frac12; m v<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> &rArr; <b>u<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> = v<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> + v<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup></b> &nbsp;&hellip;(2)<br>Subtracting (2) from (1):<br>2 (v<sub>1</sub> &bull; v<sub>2</sub>) = 0 &rArr; v<sub>1</sub> &bull; v<sub>2</sub> = 0.<br>|v<sub>1</sub>| |v<sub>2</sub>| cos(&theta;<sub>1</sub> + &theta;<sub>2</sub>) = 0 &rArr; <b>&theta;<sub>1</sub> + &theta;<sub>2</sub> = 90&deg;</b>.<br>The particles fly apart at strictly right angles.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q69: A particle of mass m with velocity u strikes an identical stationary particle elastically in 2D. If the incident particle scatters at 30° to incident axis, find the scattering angle of the target particle and their final speeds.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Since masses are equal and collision is elastic in 2D, the scattering angles satisfy: &theta;<sub>1</sub> + &theta;<sub>2</sub> = 90&deg;.<br>Given &theta;<sub>1</sub> = 30&deg; &rArr; <b>&theta;<sub>2</sub> = 90&deg; - 30&deg; = 60&deg;</b>.<br>By momentum conservation along perpendicular y-axis:<br>m v<sub>1</sub> sin 30&deg; = m v<sub>2</sub> sin 60&deg; &rArr; v<sub>1</sub>(0.5) = v<sub>2</sub>(&radic;3 / 2) &rArr; v<sub>1</sub> = &radic;3 v<sub>2</sub>.<br>From kinetic energy conservation: u<sup>2</sup> = v<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> + v<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> = 3 v<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> + v<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup> = 4 v<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>.<br>v<sub>2</sub> = u / 2 = <b>0.50 u</b>.<br>v<sub>1</sub> = &radic;3 (u / 2) = <b>(&radic;3 / 2) u &approx; 0.866 u</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q70: A particle moves along the x-axis under a conservative force F(x) = (-k x + a x³). Find its potential energy function U(x), taking standard reference U(0) = 0.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Using U(x) - U(0) = -&int;<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> F(x') dx':<br>U(x) = -&int;<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> (-k x' + a x'<sup>3</sup>) dx' = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> (k x' - a x'<sup>3</sup>) dx'.<br>U(x) = [ &frac12; k x'<sup>2</sup> - &frac14; a x'<sup>4</sup> ]<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup>.<br><b>U(x) = &frac12; k x<sup>2</sup> - &frac14; a x<sup>4</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q71: Explain why a rubber ball rebounds high after striking the floor, whereas a lump of soft wet clay sticks dead to the floor upon impact.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>Rubber Ball:</b> Exhibits high elasticity (e &approx; 0.8 - 0.9). During impact, kinetic energy is stored temporarily as elastic strain energy and almost fully restored as kinetic energy upon expansion.<br><b>Wet Clay:</b> Undergoes permanent plastic deformation (e = 0). The work of impact is completely dissipated in shearing and deforming clay molecules into internal thermal energy, resulting in zero rebound.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q72: A force of 20 N compresses a spring by 0.1 m. Calculate: (a) Spring constant k, (b) Stored elastic energy, (c) Speed with which a 0.2 kg ball placed on the spring is launched when released.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>(a) Spring Constant:</b> k = F / x = 20 / 0.1 = <b>200 N m<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br><b>(b) Stored Energy:</b> U<sub>s</sub> = &frac12; k x<sup>2</sup> = &frac12;(200)(0.1)<sup>2</sup> = 100 &times; 0.01 = <b>1.0 Joule</b>.<br><b>(c) Launch Speed:</b> All potential energy converts to kinetic energy: &frac12; m v<sup>2</sup> = U<sub>s</sub>.<br>&frac12;(0.2) v<sup>2</sup> = 1.0 &rArr; 0.1 v<sup>2</sup> = 1.0 &rArr; v<sup>2</sup> = 10 &rArr; <b>v = &radic;10 &approx; 3.16 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q73: Can a physical body possess energy without possessing linear momentum? Can a body possess linear momentum without possessing energy? Justify both cases.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Energy without Momentum: YES.</b> A body at rest (v = 0) has zero linear momentum (p = 0), but possesses gravitational potential energy (mgh) or internal rest-mass energy (E = mc²).<br><b>2. Momentum without Energy: NO.</b> If a body has momentum (p = mv &ne; 0), it must possess speed v &ne; 0, which guarantees non-zero kinetic energy (K = p²/2m > 0).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q74: An engine of mass 50 metric tons pulls a train of 200 metric tons up an incline of 1 in 100 at a steady speed of 36 km/h. If frictional resistance is 5 N per ton, calculate the power of the engine (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Total mass M = 50 + 200 = 250 tons = 250,000 kg.<br>Speed v = 36 km/h = 10 m/s. Slope sin &theta; = 1/100.<br>&bull; Downward gravitational component: F<sub>g</sub> = M g sin &theta; = 250000 &times; 9.8 &times; (1/100) = <b>24,500 N</b>.<br>&bull; Total friction: F<sub>f</sub> = 250 tons &times; 5 N/ton = <b>1,250 N</b>.<br>Total tractive force required: F = F<sub>g</sub> + F<sub>f</sub> = 24500 + 1250 = <b>25,750 N</b>.<br>Power: P = F &times; v = 25750 &times; 10 = <b>257,500 W = 257.5 kW &approx; 345 hp</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q75: Explain why the brake drums and wheels of a truck become scalding hot during a continuous steep mountain descent, in terms of work and energy transformation.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      As the truck descends, it loses gravitational potential energy &Delta;U = mgh. To maintain constant safe speed without accelerating, the brakes apply continuous retarding friction. By the Work-Energy Theorem and First Law of Thermodynamics, the non-conservative work done by friction transforms this immense mechanical potential energy into thermal internal energy in the brake pads and drums.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q76: A light body and a heavy body have equal linear momenta. Which one possesses greater kinetic energy? Prove mathematically.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Kinetic energy is K = p<sup>2</sup> / (2m).<br>Given p<sub>light</sub> = p<sub>heavy</sub> = p.<br>K<sub>light</sub> / K<sub>heavy</sub> = [p<sup>2</sup> / (2m<sub>light</sub>)] / [p<sup>2</sup> / (2m<sub>heavy</sub>)] = <b>m<sub>heavy</sub> / m<sub>light</sub></b>.<br>Since m<sub>heavy</sub> > m<sub>light</sub>, it follows that: <b>K<sub>light</sub> > K<sub>heavy</sub></b>.<br>The lighter body has significantly greater kinetic energy.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q77: A light body and a heavy body have identical kinetic energies. Which one possesses greater linear momentum? Prove mathematically.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Momentum is p = &radic;(2m K).<br>Given K<sub>light</sub> = K<sub>heavy</sub> = K.<br>p<sub>heavy</sub> / p<sub>light</sub> = &radic;(2m<sub>heavy</sub> K) / &radic;(2m<sub>light</sub> K) = <b>&radic;(m<sub>heavy</sub> / m<sub>light</sub>)</b>.<br>Since m<sub>heavy</sub> > m<sub>light</sub>, it follows that: <b>p<sub>heavy</sub> > p<sub>light</sub></b>.<br>The heavier body carries greater linear momentum.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q78: What is a Conservative Field? Prove that the Earth's gravitational field is conservative.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      A force field is conservative if the line integral of the force along any closed path is zero: &oint; F &bull; dr = 0.<br>For gravity near Earth, F = -mg ĵ.<br>For any path from (x<sub>1</sub>, y<sub>1</sub>) to (x<sub>2</sub>, y<sub>2</sub>):<br>W = &int; (-mg ĵ) &bull; (dx î + dy ĵ) = -&int;<sub>y<sub>1</sub></sub><sup>y<sub>2</sub></sup> mg dy = -mg(y<sub>2</sub> - y<sub>1</sub>).<br>The work depends solely on initial and final vertical coordinates and is independent of horizontal path. For a closed loop, y<sub>2</sub> = y<sub>1</sub> &rArr; W = 0, proving gravity is strictly conservative.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q79: An athlete of mass 60 kg climbs a vertical training rope of length 10 meters in 15 seconds at uniform speed. Calculate the power developed by the athlete (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      m = 60 kg, h = 10 m, t = 15 s, g = 9.8 m/s².<br>Work done against gravity: W = m g h = 60 &times; 9.8 &times; 10 = <b>5,880 Joules</b>.<br>Power developed: P = W / t = 5880 / 15 = <b>392 Watts &approx; 0.525 hp</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q80: Describe the continuous transformation between kinetic and potential energy in an oscillating simple pendulum. Show how total mechanical energy remains conserved at the mean and extreme positions.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Let the pendulum bob of mass m oscillate with amplitude displacement h.<br>&bull; <b>At Extreme Position:</b> Velocity vanishes momentarily (v = 0 &rArr; K = 0). Height is maximum (U = mgh). Total energy: <b>E<sub>ext</sub> = mgh</b>.<br>&bull; <b>At Mean Equilibrium Position:</b> Height is datum level (h = 0 &rArr; U = 0). Velocity is maximum (v = &radic;(2gh)). Kinetic energy: K = &frac12;m(2gh) = mgh. Total energy: <b>E<sub>mean</sub> = mgh</b>.<br>&bull; <b>At Intermediate Position:</b> Mechanical energy is partitioned: E = &frac12;mv<sup>2</sup> + mgy = mgh.<br>Energy continuously shifts between potential and kinetic forms while total mechanical energy remains perfectly invariant.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION C: LA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #00c6ff; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section C: Long Answer (LA) Questions (5 Marks each) &bull; Q81 to Q100\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Comprehensive multi-step board derivations (Work-Energy Theorem for variable force, spring potential energy, vertical circle dynamics, 1D & 2D collisions, and master unit synthesis).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q81: State and prove the Work-Energy Theorem for a Variable Force in one dimension using the definite calculus integration method.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Statement of the Work-Energy Theorem:</b><br>\n      The net work done by all forces acting on an object during any displacement is identically equal to the change in the kinetic energy of the object: <b>W = K_f - K_i = &Delta;K</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Mathematical Calculus Proof:</b><br>\n      Consider a body of mass m moving along the x-axis under the action of a position-dependent variable force F(x).<br>\n      By Newton's Second Law: F(x) = m a = m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Applying the calculus chain rule for acceleration:<br>\n      a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span> cdot <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dx</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = v , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Therefore: F(x) = m v , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Rearranging differential terms: F(x) , dx = m v , dv.<br><br>\n      The total mechanical work done by the variable force as the particle moves from initial position x_1 (where velocity is v_1) to final position x_2 (where velocity is v_2) is the definite integral:<br>\n      W = int_{x_1}<sup>x_2</sup> F(x) , dx = int_{v_1}<sup>v_2</sup> m v , dv.<br>\n      Since mass m is constant, it factors out of the integral:<br>\n      W = m int_{v_1}<sup>v_2</sup> v , dv = m left[ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> \right]_{v_1}<sup>v_2</sup> = m left( <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v_2<sup>2</sup> - v_1<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> \right).<br>\n      Expanding the brackets:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        W = \frac{1}{2} m v_2<sup>2</sup> - \frac{1}{2} m v_1<sup>2</sup> = K_f - K_i = Delta K\n      </div>\n      This rigorously establishes that the work done by a variable force equals the net change in kinetic energy.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q82: Derive an expression for the Elastic Potential Energy stored in a helical spring stretched by a distance x. Graphically illustrate the force-displacement relation, and distinguish between work done by the spring force and work done by the external pulling agent.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Physical Mechanism & Hooke's Law:</b><br>\n      Consider a massless helical spring of stiffness constant k lying on a smooth horizontal table with one end fixed to a rigid wall and the other attached to a mass m.<br>\n      When stretched by displacement x from natural equilibrium (x = 0), the spring exerts an internal restoring force directed toward the origin: <b>F_s = -k x</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Derivation of Potential Energy:</b><br>\n      To stretch the spring slowly without acceleration, the external applied pulling force must balance the restoring force at every instant: F_{ext} = -F_s = +kx.<br>\n      The work done by the external pulling force in producing an infinitesimal elongation dx is:<br>\n      dW_{ext} = F_{ext} , dx = (kx) , dx.<br>\n      Total work done in stretching the spring from unstrained state x = 0 to final extension x is:<br>\n      W_{ext} = int<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> k x' , dx' = k left[ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">x'<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2</span>\n  </span> \right]<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> = \frac{1}{2} k x<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      By definition, this work is stored entirely as internal elastic strain potential energy (U_s):<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        U_s = \frac{1}{2} k x<sup>2</sup>\n      </div>\n      <b>3. Work Done by Spring Force vs External Force:</b><br>\n      &bull; Work done by external pulling agent: W_{ext} = +\frac{1}{2}kx<sup>2</sup> (positive, as F_{ext} and displacement are in the same direction).<br>\n      &bull; Work done by internal spring restoring force: W_s = int<sub>0</sub><sup>x</sup> (-kx) dx = -\frac{1}{2}kx<sup>2</sup> = -Delta U_s (negative, as restoring force opposes elongation).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q83: State the Principle of Conservation of Mechanical Energy. Prove analytically that the total mechanical energy of a freely falling body of mass m dropped from height H is strictly conserved at all points of its descent.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Statement:</b><br>\n      The total mechanical energy (sum of kinetic energy K and potential energy U) of an isolated system remains strictly constant over time if the internal forces doing work are conservative: E = K + U = constant.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Analytical Proof for Free Fall under Gravity:</b><br>\n      Let a body of mass m be dropped from rest from initial height H above the ground. Take ground as reference zero datum (U = 0 at y = 0).<br><br>\n      &bull; <b>At Initial Position A (Height y = H):</b><br>\n      Velocity v_A = 0 implies K_A = \frac{1}{2}m(0)<sup>2</sup> = 0.<br>\n      Potential energy: U_A = mgH.<br>\n      Total mechanical energy: <b>E_A = K_A + U_A = 0 + mgH = mgH &nbsp;&hellip;(1)</b><br><br>\n      &bull; <b>At Intermediate Position B (Fallen distance x, Height y = H - x):</b><br>\n      Using third equation of motion: v_B<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2gx = 0 + 2gx = 2gx.<br>\n      Kinetic energy: K_B = \frac{1}{2}m v_B<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2}m(2gx) = mgx.<br>\n      Potential energy: U_B = mg(H - x) = mgH - mgx.<br>\n      Total mechanical energy: <b>E_B = K_B + U_B = mgx + (mgH - mgx) = mgH &nbsp;&hellip;(2)</b><br><br>\n      &bull; <b>At Ground Impact Position C (Height y = 0):</b><br>\n      Using v_C<sup>2</sup> = 0 + 2gH = 2gH.<br>\n      Kinetic energy: K_C = \frac{1}{2}m v_C<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2}m(2gH) = mgH.<br>\n      Potential energy: U_C = mg(0) = 0.<br>\n      Total mechanical energy: <b>E_C = K_C + U_C = mgH + 0 = mgH &nbsp;&hellip;(3)</b><br><br>\n      <b>Conclusion:</b> Comparing equations (1), (2), and (3):<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        E_A = E_B = E_C = mgH = \text{Constant}\n      </div>\n      As the body descends, potential energy continuously transforms into kinetic energy, but their sum remains constant.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q84: A small body of mass m is whirled in a vertical circle by means of a light string of length L. Derive expressions for: (a) String tension T(&theta;) at any angle &theta;, (b) Minimum critical velocity at the top (v_top), (c) Minimum velocity at the bottom (v_bottom) required to execute the full circle.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Force Resolution at Angle &theta; with Downward Vertical:</b><br>\n      Let the body possess speed v when the string is inclined at angle \theta to the downward vertical.<br>\n      Forces acting on the mass:<br>\n      1. Tension T directed radially inward along the string.<br>\n      2. Weight mg directed vertically downward, resolving into radial component mgcos\theta (outward) and tangential component mgsin\theta (opposing motion).<br>\n      Net radial centripetal force: T - mgcos\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        T(\theta) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + mg cos\theta\n      </div>\n      <b>(a) Minimum Velocity at Highest Point (Crest, &theta; = 180&deg;):</b><br>\n      At the peak, cos 180<sup>c</sup>irc = -1 implies T_{top} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{top}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> - mg.<br>\n      To execute the loop without the string going slack, tension must be non-negative: T_{top} ge 0.<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{top}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> - mg ge 0 implies v_{top}<sup>2</sup> ge gL.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_{top, min} = sqrt{g L}\n      </div>\n      <b>(b) Minimum Velocity at Lowest Point (Trough, &theta; = 0&deg;):</b><br>\n      By conservation of mechanical energy between the bottom (height 0) and top (height 2L):<br>\n      \frac{1}{2}m v_{bottom}<sup>2</sup> + 0 = \frac{1}{2}m v_{top}<sup>2</sup> + mg(2L).<br>\n      Substitute the critical top speed v_{top}<sup>2</sup> = gL:<br>\n      \frac{1}{2}m v_{bottom}<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2}m(gL) + 2mgL = \frac{5}{2}mgL.<br>\n      Multiplying by 2/m:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_{bottom, min} = sqrt{5 g L}\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q85: In vertical circular motion, prove analytically that: (a) The string tension at the lowest point exceeds that at the highest point by an invariant constant 6 mg, and (b) Derive the critical speed at the horizontal mid-point (v_mid = &radic;(3 g L)).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>(a) Proof that T_bottom - T_top = 6 mg:</b><br>\n      Using the tension formula T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + mgcos\theta:<br>\n      &bull; At lowest point (\theta = 0<sup>c</sup>irc, cos 0<sup>c</sup>irc = 1):<br>\n      T_{bottom} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_b<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + mg quad ldots(1)<br>\n      &bull; At highest point (\theta = 180<sup>c</sup>irc, cos 180<sup>c</sup>irc = -1):<br>\n      T_{top} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_t<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> - mg quad ldots(2)<br>\n      Subtracting (2) from (1):<br>\n      T_{bottom} - T_{top} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span>(v_b<sup>2</sup> - v_t<sup>2</sup>) + 2mg quad ldots(3)<br><br>\n      By conservation of mechanical energy between trough and crest:<br>\n      \frac{1}{2}m v_b<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2}m v_t<sup>2</sup> + mg(2L) implies v_b<sup>2</sup> - v_t<sup>2</sup> = 4gL.<br>\n      Substitute (v_b<sup>2</sup> - v_t<sup>2</sup>) = 4gL into equation (3):<br>\n      T_{bottom} - T_{top} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span>(4gL) + 2mg = 4mg + 2mg = mathbf{6mg}.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        T_{bottom} - T_{top} = 6 mg\n      </div>\n      <b>(b) Critical Speed at Horizontal Mid-Point (&theta; = 90&deg;):</b><br>\n      At the horizontal position, height is L above the bottom datum.<br>\n      By conservation of energy between bottom and mid-point:<br>\n      \frac{1}{2}m v_b<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2}m v_{mid}<sup>2</sup> + mgL.<br>\n      Substitute critical bottom velocity v_b<sup>2</sup> = 5gL:<br>\n      \frac{1}{2}m(5gL) = \frac{1}{2}m v_{mid}<sup>2</sup> + mgL implies 5gL = v_{mid}<sup>2</sup> + 2gL.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_{mid} = sqrt{3 g L} quad \text{and} quad T_{mid} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m(3gL)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">L</span>\n  </span> + 0 = 3mg\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q86: Derive the complete mathematical theory of a One-Dimensional Head-On Perfectly Elastic Collision: Prove that the relative velocity of approach equals the relative velocity of separation, and derive the closed-form formulas for the final velocities v_1 and v_2.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Physical Setup & Invariant Conservation Laws:</b><br>\n      Let two particles of masses m_1 and m_2 move along the x-axis with initial velocities u_1 and u_2 (u_1 > u_2). They collide head-on elastically, emerging with velocities v_1 and v_2.<br><br>\n      <b>Law 1: Conservation of Linear Momentum:</b><br>\n      m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2 = m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2<br>\n      Rearranging terms: m_1(u_1 - v_1) = m_2(v_2 - u_2) quad ldots(1)<br><br>\n      <b>Law 2: Conservation of Kinetic Energy:</b><br>\n      \frac{1}{2}m_1 u_1<sup>2</sup> + \frac{1}{2}m_2 u_2<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2}m_1 v_1<sup>2</sup> + \frac{1}{2}m_2 v_2<sup>2</sup><br>\n      Rearranging terms: m_1(u_1<sup>2</sup> - v_1<sup>2</sup>) = m_2(v_2<sup>2</sup> - u_2<sup>2</sup>) quad ldots(2)<br><br>\n      <b>2. Proof that Relative Approach = Relative Separation:</b><br>\n      Factor equation (2) using (a<sup>2</sup> - b<sup>2</sup>) = (a - b)(a + b):<br>\n      m_1(u_1 - v_1)(u_1 + v_1) = m_2(v_2 - u_2)(v_2 + u_2) quad ldots(3)<br>\n      Dividing equation (3) by equation (1):<br>\n      u_1 + v_1 = v_2 + u_2 implies mathbf{u_1 - u_2 = v_2 - v_1}.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \text{Velocity of Approach } (u_1 - u_2) = \text{Velocity of Separation } (v_2 - v_1) implies e = 1\n      </div>\n      <b>3. Closed-Form Final Velocity Formulas:</b><br>\n      From above, v_2 = u_1 - u_2 + v_1. Substitute this into momentum equation (1):<br>\n      m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2 = m_1 v_1 + m_2(u_1 - u_2 + v_1) = m_1 v_1 + m_2 u_1 - m_2 u_2 + m_2 v_1<br>\n      (m_1 + m_2) v_1 = (m_1 - m_2) u_1 + 2 m_2 u_2.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_1 = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 - m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) u_1 + left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) u_2\n      </div>\n      Similarly, substituting v_1 = v_2 - (u_1 - u_2) yields:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_2 = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) u_1 + left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_2 - m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) u_2\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q87: For a 1D elastic collision, analyze the three classic boundary cases in detail: (a) Equal masses (m_1 = m_2), (b) Massive projectile striking light stationary target (m_1 >> m_2, u_2 = 0), (c) Light projectile striking massive stationary target (m_1 << m_2, u_2 = 0).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>Using Master Velocity Formulas:</b><br>\n      v_1 = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 - m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right)u_1 + left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right)u_2<br>\n      v_2 = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right)u_1 + left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_2 - m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right)u_2<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Case I: Identical Masses (m_1 = m_2 = m):</b><br>\n      v_1 = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">0</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2m</span>\n  </span>\right)u_1 + left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2m</span>\n  </span>\right)u_2 = mathbf{u_2}.<br>\n      v_2 = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2m</span>\n  </span>\right)u_1 + left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">0</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2m</span>\n  </span>\right)u_2 = mathbf{u_1}.<br>\n      <b>Physical Result:</b> Two colliding bodies of equal mass completely exchange their velocities upon 1D elastic collision. If target m_2 was initially at rest (u_2 = 0), projectile m_1 stops dead (v_1 = 0) while target m_2 moves off with original speed u_1.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Case II: Massive Projectile striking Light Stationary Target (m_1 gg m_2, u_2 = 0):</b><br>\n      Neglecting m_2 in comparison to m_1 (m_1 - m_2 approx m_1, m_1 + m_2 approx m_1):<br>\n      v_1 approx left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1</span>\n  </span>\right) u_1 = mathbf{u_1} (heavy projectile continues forward virtually unperturbed).<br>\n      v_2 approx left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1</span>\n  </span>\right) u_1 = mathbf{2 u_1} (light target is launched forward at double the projectile speed!).<br><br>\n      <b>(c) Case III: Light Projectile striking Massive Stationary Target (m_1 ll m_2, u_2 = 0):</b><br>\n      Neglecting m_1 in comparison to m_2 (m_1 - m_2 approx -m_2, m_1 + m_2 approx m_2):<br>\n      v_1 approx left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">-m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) u_1 = mathbf{-u_1} (light projectile rebounds backward with identical speed).<br>\n      v_2 approx left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2(0)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) u_1 = mathbf{0} (massive target remains stationary, like a tennis ball hitting a brick wall).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q88: Derive an analytical formula for the Loss of Kinetic Energy in a Completely Inelastic Collision in One Dimension. Prove that kinetic energy is always lost (&Delta;K > 0) in such an impact.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Common Velocity Derivation:</b><br>\n      Let two bodies of masses m_1 and m_2 move with initial velocities u_1 and u_2.<br>\n      In a completely inelastic collision, the bodies stick together and move with a single common velocity V.<br>\n      By conservation of linear momentum:<br>\n      m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2 = (m_1 + m_2) V implies mathbf{V = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Kinetic Energy Before and After:</b><br>\n      Initial kinetic energy: K_i = \frac{1}{2}m_1 u_1<sup>2</sup> + \frac{1}{2}m_2 u_2<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      Final kinetic energy: K_f = \frac{1}{2}(m_1 + m_2) V<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2}(m_1 + m_2) left[ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span> \right]<sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">(m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2)<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2(m_1 + m_2)</span>\n  </span>.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Loss of Kinetic Energy (Delta K = K_i - K_f):</b><br>\n      Delta K = left(\frac{1}{2}m_1 u_1<sup>2</sup> + \frac{1}{2}m_2 u_2<sup>2</sup>\right) - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">(m_1 u_1 + m_2 u_2)<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2(m_1 + m_2)</span>\n  </span><br>\n      Delta K = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 u_1<sup>2</sup>(m_1 + m_2) + m_2 u_2<sup>2</sup>(m_1 + m_2) - (m_1<sup>2</sup> u_1<sup>2</sup> + m_2<sup>2</sup> u_2<sup>2</sup> + 2m_1 m_2 u_1 u_2)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2(m_1 + m_2)</span>\n  </span><br>\n      Delta K = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1<sup>2</sup> u_1<sup>2</sup> + m_1 m_2 u_1<sup>2</sup> + m_1 m_2 u_2<sup>2</sup> + m_2<sup>2</sup> u_2<sup>2</sup> - m_1<sup>2</sup> u_1<sup>2</sup> - m_2<sup>2</sup> u_2<sup>2</sup> - 2m_1 m_2 u_1 u_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2(m_1 + m_2)</span>\n  </span><br>\n      Cancelling like terms and factoring m_1 m_2:<br>\n      Delta K = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 m_2 (u_1<sup>2</sup> + u_2<sup>2</sup> - 2u_1 u_2)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2(m_1 + m_2)</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        Delta K = \frac{1}{2} left( <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span> \right) (u_1 - u_2)<sup>2</sup>\n      </div>\n      <b>Proof that Energy is Always Lost:</b><br>\n      Since (m_1 + m_2) > 0, m_1 m_2 > 0, and (u_1 - u_2)<sup>2</sup> > 0 for any relative velocity, Delta K is <b>strictly positive (Delta K > 0)</b>. Kinetic energy is permanently dissipated into heat, sound, and plastic work.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q89: Formulate the theory of an Elastic Collision in Two Dimensions (Glancing Collision). Prove analytically that when two particles of identical mass collide elastically with one initially at rest, they scatter at strictly 90° to each other.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Mathematical Formulation:</b><br>\n      Let projectile mass m_1 moving along x-axis with velocity u_1 collide elastically with stationary target mass m_2 (u_2 = 0).<br>\n      After collision, m_1 moves with speed v_1 at angle \theta_1 above the x-axis, and m_2 moves with speed v_2 at angle \theta_2 below the x-axis.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Momentum conservation along x-axis:</b> m_1 u_1 = m_1 v_1 cos\theta_1 + m_2 v_2 cos\theta_2 quad ldots(1)<br>\n      &bull; <b>Momentum conservation along y-axis:</b> 0 = m_1 v_1 sin\theta_1 - m_2 v_2 sin\theta_2 quad ldots(2)<br>\n      &bull; <b>Kinetic energy conservation:</b> \frac{1}{2}m_1 u_1<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2}m_1 v_1<sup>2</sup> + \frac{1}{2}m_2 v_2<sup>2</sup> quad ldots(3)<br><br>\n      <b>2. Analytical Proof of Right-Angle Scattering (m_1 = m_2 = m):</b><br>\n      Cancelling mass m in vector momentum conservation:<br>\n      \u000bec{u}_1 = \u000bec{v}_1 + \u000bec{v}_2.<br>\n      Taking the vector self-dot product:<br>\n      |\u000bec{u}_1|<sup>2</sup> = (\u000bec{v}_1 + \u000bec{v}_2) cdot (\u000bec{v}_1 + \u000bec{v}_2) = v_1<sup>2</sup> + v_2<sup>2</sup> + 2 (\u000bec{v}_1 cdot \u000bec{v}_2) quad ldots(4)<br>\n      From kinetic energy conservation with m_1 = m_2:<br>\n      u_1<sup>2</sup> = v_1<sup>2</sup> + v_2<sup>2</sup> quad ldots(5)<br>\n      Comparing (4) and (5):<br>\n      v_1<sup>2</sup> + v_2<sup>2</sup> + 2 (\u000bec{v}_1 cdot \u000bec{v}_2) = v_1<sup>2</sup> + v_2<sup>2</sup> implies 2 (\u000bec{v}_1 cdot \u000bec{v}_2) = 0 implies \u000bec{v}_1 cdot \u000bec{v}_2 = 0.<br>\n      By definition of dot product:<br>\n      v_1 v_2 cos(\theta_1 + \theta_2) = 0 implies cos(\theta_1 + \theta_2) = 0 implies mathbf{\theta_1 + \theta_2 = 90<sup>c</sup>irc}.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \theta_1 + \theta_2 = 90<sup>c</sup>irc quad \text{(Orthogonal Scattering)}\n      </div>\n      <i>The two identical particles fly apart at strictly right angles.</i>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q90: Explain the operating physics of the Ballistic Pendulum. Derive the formula used to determine the high speed of a rifle bullet from the observable vertical swing height h of the pendulum block.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Physical Operation:</b><br>\n      A ballistic pendulum is a heavy wooden block of mass M suspended by light strings. A high-speed bullet of mass m is fired horizontally into the block and embeds inside it. The collision is completely inelastic. Thereafter, the combined system swings upward as a pendulum, converting kinetic energy into gravitational potential energy.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Stage 1: Inelastic Impact (Momentum Conserved):</b><br>\n      Let bullet strike with muzzle speed u.<br>\n      Initial momentum: P_i = m u.<br>\n      Final momentum immediately after embedment: P_f = (m + M) V.<br>\n      By conservation of linear momentum:<br>\n      m u = (m + M) V implies mathbf{V = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m + M</span>\n  </span>\right) u}.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Stage 2: Pendulum Swing (Mechanical Energy Conserved):</b><br>\n      As the pendulum swings up through vertical height h, all kinetic energy converts to potential energy:<br>\n      \frac{1}{2} (m + M) V<sup>2</sup> = (m + M) g h implies V<sup>2</sup> = 2 g h implies mathbf{V = sqrt{2 g h}}.<br><br>\n      <b>4. Formula for Bullet Speed:</b><br>\n      Equating the two expressions for common velocity V:<br>\n      left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m + M</span>\n  </span>\right) u = sqrt{2 g h}.<br>\n      Solving for bullet velocity u:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        u = left( <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m + M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m</span>\n  </span> \right) sqrt{2 g h}\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q91: Define Power. Distinguish between Average Power and Instantaneous Power. Derive the relation P = F &bull; v, and calculate the power of a crane that lifts a 2.5-ton load at a constant speed of 1.2 m/s against gravity (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Definitions:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Power:</b> The time rate of doing mechanical work or energy consumption: P = dW/dt.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Average Power (P_{avg}):</b> Total work performed divided by total elapsed time: P_{avg} = Delta W / Delta t.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Instantaneous Power (P):</b> Rate of work at a specific instant: P = lim_{Delta t \to 0} \frac{Delta W}{Delta t} = \frac{dW}{dt}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Derivation of P = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{v}:</b><br>\n      Infinitesimal work done by force \u000bec{F} in displacement d\u000bec{r} is: dW = \u000bec{F} cdot d\u000bec{r}.<br>\n      Dividing by time differential dt:<br>\n      P = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dW</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = \u000bec{F} cdot <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d\u000bec{r}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Since \frac{d\u000bec{r}}{dt} = \u000bec{v} (instantaneous velocity vector):<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        P = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{v} = F v cos\theta\n      </div>\n      <b>3. Crane Numerical Calculation:</b><br>\n      Mass m = 2.5\text{ metric tons} = 2500\text{ kg}, v = 1.2\text{ m/s}, g = 9.8\text{ m/s}<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      Because moving at constant speed, upward crane force equals downward gravitational weight:<br>\n      F = mg = 2500 \times 9.8 = 24,500\text{ N}.<br>\n      Since \u000bec{F} and \u000bec{v} are both vertically upward, \theta = 0<sup>c</sup>irc implies cos 0<sup>c</sup>irc = 1.<br>\n      P = F \times v = 24500 \times 1.2 = mathbf{29,400\text{ Watts} = 29.4\text{ kW}}.<br>\n      In Horsepower: P = 29400 / 746 approx mathbf{39.4\text{ hp}}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q92: Discuss the relationship between a Conservative Force and its Potential Energy: Derive F = -dU/dx, and prove that the work done by any conservative force around any arbitrary closed loop is identically zero (&oint; F &bull; dr = 0).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Potential Energy Definition:</b><br>\n      Potential energy is defined such that the change in potential energy during an infinitesimal displacement dx equals the negative of work done by the conservative force:<br>\n      dU = -dW_{cons} = -F(x) , dx implies mathbf{F(x) = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dU</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span>}.<br>\n      In 3D Cartesian coordinates: \u000bec{F} = -\nabla U = -left( \frac{partial U}{partial x}hat{i} + \frac{partial U}{partial y}hat{j} + \frac{partial U}{partial z}hat{k} \right).<br><br>\n      <b>2. Proof of Path Independence:</b><br>\n      Work done in moving from point A to point B along any path is:<br>\n      W_{A \to B} = int_A<sup>B</sup> \u000bec{F} cdot d\u000bec{r} = -int_A<sup>B</sup> dU = -(U_B - U_A) = U_A - U_B.<br>\n      Because this depends exclusively on potential energies at endpoints A and B, the work is strictly independent of the intermediate path.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Proof that Closed Loop Work is Zero (&oint; F &bull; dr = 0):</b><br>\n      Consider a closed path starting at point A, traversing to point B via path 1, and returning from B to A via path 2:<br>\n      oint \u000bec{F} cdot d\u000bec{r} = int_{A(1)}<sup>B</sup> \u000bec{F} cdot d\u000bec{r} + int_{B(2)}<sup>A</sup> \u000bec{F} cdot d\u000bec{r}.<br>\n      Using endpoint potential energy values:<br>\n      oint \u000bec{F} cdot d\u000bec{r} = (U_A - U_B) + (U_B - U_A) = mathbf{0}.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        oint \u000bec{F}_{cons} cdot d\u000bec{r} = 0\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q93: Using the Work-Energy Theorem, derive the expression for the final speed of a block of mass m sliding down a rough inclined plane of length s, inclination angle &theta;, and kinetic friction coefficient &mu;_k.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Force Analysis on Rough Incline:</b><br>\n      A block of mass m starts from rest (u = 0) at the top of an incline of length s and height h = ssin\theta.<br>\n      Forces acting on the block:<br>\n      1. Gravity mg acting vertically downward.<br>\n      2. Normal reaction N = mgcos\theta acting perpendicular to incline.<br>\n      3. Kinetic friction f_k = mu_k N = mu_k mgcos\theta acting upward along the incline.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Calculation of Work Done by All Forces:</b><br>\n      &bull; Work done by gravity: W_g = (mgsin\theta) \times s = mgh.<br>\n      &bull; Work done by normal reaction: W_N = 0 (since \u000bec{N} perp \u000bec{s}).<br>\n      &bull; Work done by friction: W_f = -f_k \times s = -(mu_k mgcos\theta) s.<br>\n      Total net work done:<br>\n      W_{net} = W_g + W_f = mg s sin\theta - mu_k mg s cos\theta = mg s (sin\theta - mu_k cos\theta).<br><br>\n      <b>3. Application of Work-Energy Theorem:</b><br>\n      W_{net} = K_f - K_i = \frac{1}{2} m v<sup>2</sup> - 0 = \frac{1}{2} m v<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      Equating expressions:<br>\n      \frac{1}{2} m v<sup>2</sup> = mg s (sin\theta - mu_k cos\theta).<br>\n      Cancelling m and solving for final speed v:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v = sqrt{2 g s (sin\theta - mu_k cos\theta)}\n      </div>\n      If the incline is perfectly smooth (mu_k = 0), the formula reduces to v = sqrt{2gssin\theta} = sqrt{2gh}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q94: A heavy truck of mass m climbs a hill of inclination &theta; with constant speed v. If coefficient of rolling friction is &mu; and aerodynamic air resistance is F_air, derive the total power delivered by the truck engine.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Force Balance along the Incline:</b><br>\n      To maintain constant velocity (a = 0), the forward tractive force F_{engine} exerted by the wheels on the road must balance all opposing resistive forces.<br>\n      Opposing forces acting downward along the incline:<br>\n      1. Downward gravitational component: F_g = mg sin\theta.<br>\n      2. Rolling friction: F_f = mu N = mu mg cos\theta.<br>\n      3. Aerodynamic drag: F_{air}.<br><br>\n      Total forward force required:<br>\n      F_{engine} = mg sin\theta + mu mg cos\theta + F_{air} = mg(sin\theta + mu cos\theta) + F_{air}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Power Output Formulation:</b><br>\n      Power delivered at velocity v is P = F_{engine} \times v:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        P = left[ mg(sin\theta + mu cos\theta) + F_{air} \right] v\n      </div>\n      This formula accounts for gravitational lifting work, frictional dissipation, and aerodynamic drag.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q95: Formulate the Generalized Work-Energy Theorem for systems containing both conservative and non-conservative forces. Prove that the work done by non-conservative forces equals the net change in total mechanical energy (W_nc = &Delta;E).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Separation of Forces:</b><br>\n      The total net force acting on an object is the sum of conservative forces and non-conservative forces:<br>\n      \u000bec{F}_{net} = \u000bec{F}_{cons} + \u000bec{F}_{non-cons}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Work Decomposition:</b><br>\n      Total work done is the sum of conservative and non-conservative work:<br>\n      W_{net} = W_{cons} + W_{nc}.<br>\n      By the fundamental Work-Energy Theorem: W_{net} = Delta K.<br>\n      Therefore: W_{cons} + W_{nc} = Delta K quad ldots(1)<br><br>\n      <b>3. Relation to Potential Energy:</b><br>\n      By definition of potential energy: W_{cons} = -Delta U.<br>\n      Substitute W_{cons} = -Delta U into equation (1):<br>\n      -Delta U + W_{nc} = Delta K implies W_{nc} = Delta K + Delta U.<br>\n      Since total mechanical energy is E = K + U, we have Delta E = Delta K + Delta U.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        W_{nc} = Delta E = (K_f + U_f) - (K_i + U_i)\n      </div>\n      <b>Conclusion:</b> In the presence of friction or air drag, the total mechanical energy decreases by an amount exactly equal to the negative work performed by those non-conservative forces.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q96: Discuss the three states of mechanical equilibrium (stable, unstable, and neutral) in terms of potential energy U(x), its first derivative dU/dx, and its second derivative d²U/dx².</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Equilibrium Condition:</b><br>\n      At equilibrium, net force must be zero: F = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dU</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span> = 0 implies mathbf{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dU</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span> = 0}.<br>\n      Equilibrium states correspond to the stationary points (extrema) of the potential energy curve.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Three Equilibrium Classifications:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>1. Stable Equilibrium (Local Minimum of U):</b><br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dU</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span> = 0 quad \text{and} quad mathbf{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<sup>2</sup>U</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span> > 0}.<br>\n      Potential energy is at a minimum. When displaced slightly, the force F = -dU/dx acts in the opposite direction, restoring the body to equilibrium. <i>Example:</i> A marble at the bottom of a spherical bowl.<br><br>\n      &bull; <b>2. Unstable Equilibrium (Local Maximum of U):</b><br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dU</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span> = 0 quad \text{and} quad mathbf{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<sup>2</sup>U</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span> < 0}.<br>\n      Potential energy is at a maximum. When displaced, the force accelerates the body further away from equilibrium. <i>Example:</i> A marble balanced precariously on the apex of an inverted bowl.<br><br>\n      &bull; <b>3. Neutral Equilibrium (Constant Plateau of U):</b><br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dU</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx</span>\n  </span> = 0 quad \text{and} quad mathbf{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<sup>2</sup>U</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dx<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span> = 0}.<br>\n      Potential energy is uniform. When displaced, no net force arises, and the body remains in equilibrium at its new position. <i>Example:</i> A marble resting on a flat horizontal table.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q97: A block attached to a spring of constant k is compressed by x_0 on a smooth floor and released toward a rough horizontal strip of length d and friction &mu;. Derive the stopping condition using energy methods.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Energy Accounting:</b><br>\n      Initial state: Block held at compression x_0.<br>\n      Initial mechanical energy: E_i = \frac{1}{2} k x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      When released, all elastic energy converts into kinetic energy as it passes equilibrium: K = \frac{1}{2} k x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Work Done by Friction across Rough Strip:</b><br>\n      On the rough surface, normal reaction is N = mg.<br>\n      Frictional force is f_k = mu mg.<br>\n      If the block comes to rest after sliding distance s on the rough patch:<br>\n      Work done by friction: W_f = -f_k s = -(mu mg) s.<br>\n      By the Generalized Work-Energy Theorem: W_{nc} = Delta E = 0 - E_i = -\frac{1}{2} k x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      -(mu mg) s = -\frac{1}{2} k x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      Solving for stopping distance s:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        s = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">k x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2 mu m g</span>\n  </span>\n      </div>\n      If s < d, the block stops inside the rough patch. If s > d, it exits the patch with remaining kinetic energy K_{exit} = \frac{1}{2} k x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> - mu mg d.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q98: A particle of mass m is attached to a light rigid rod of length L (instead of a flexible string) and whirled in a vertical circle. Derive the critical minimum velocities at the top and bottom, and explain why they differ from the string case.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Fundamental Physical Difference (Rod vs String):</b><br>\n      A flexible string cannot support compressive thrust; if velocity drops below sqrt{gL} at the top, tension becomes negative and the string goes slack.<br>\n      In contrast, a <b>rigid rod</b> can support compression as well as tension. The particle cannot fall inward. Therefore, to complete the vertical circle, the particle only needs to reach the topmost point with <b>infinitesimal velocity (v_{top} ge 0)</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Critical Top Velocity:</b><br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_{top, min} = 0 quad (\text{compared to } sqrt{gL} \text{ for string})\n      </div>\n      <b>3. Critical Bottom Velocity:</b><br>\n      By conservation of mechanical energy between lowest point and highest point (height 2L):<br>\n      \frac{1}{2} m v_{bottom}<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2} m v_{top}<sup>2</sup> + mg(2L).<br>\n      Substituting v_{top} = 0:<br>\n      \frac{1}{2} m v_{bottom}<sup>2</sup> = 0 + 2mgL = 2mgL implies v_{bottom}<sup>2</sup> = 4gL.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_{bottom, min} = sqrt{4 g L} = 2 sqrt{g L} quad (\text{compared to } sqrt{5gL} \text{ for string})\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q99: A bullet of mass m moving with speed u collides with a block of mass M attached to a horizontal spring of constant k on a frictionless table. Derive the maximum compression x_max of the spring when: (a) Collision is completely inelastic (bullet embeds), (b) Collision is perfectly elastic (m << M).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>(a) Inelastic Collision (Bullet Embeds):</b><br>\n      &bull; Momentum conservation: m u = (m + M) V implies V = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m + M</span>\n  </span>\right) u.<br>\n      &bull; Energy conservation during spring compression:<br>\n      \frac{1}{2} (m + M) V<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2} k x_{\\max}<sup>2</sup> implies x_{\\max} = V sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m + M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">k</span>\n  </span>}.<br>\n      Substitute V:<br>\n      x_{\\max} = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m u</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m + M</span>\n  </span>\right) sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m + M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">k</span>\n  </span>} = mathbf{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m u</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">&radic;(k(m + M))</span>\n  </span>}.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Elastic Collision (m ll M):</b><br>\n      Target M is initially at rest (u_2 = 0).<br>\n      Velocity transferred to block M in 1D elastic collision:<br>\n      V_M = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m + M</span>\n  </span>\right) u approx left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span>\right) u.<br>\n      All kinetic energy of block M converts into spring strain energy:<br>\n      \frac{1}{2} M V_M<sup>2</sup> = \frac{1}{2} k x_{\\max}<sup>2</sup> implies x_{\\max} = V_M sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">k</span>\n  </span>} = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2m u</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span>\right) sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">k</span>\n  </span>} = mathbf{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2m u</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">&radic;(k M)</span>\n  </span>}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00c6ff; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q100: Master Comprehensive Synthesis of Unit IV: Work, Energy and Power (06 Marks). Synthesize the full theoretical architecture connecting scalar work, calculus theorems, conservative potential fields, vertical circular dynamics, collision invariants, and top CBSE board examination pitfalls.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #00c6ff; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00c6ff; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Core Theoretical Architecture of Unit IV:</b><br>\n      Unit IV: Work, Energy and Power (06 Marks) unifies Newtonian dynamics through scalar energy conservation principles.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Master Operational Principles:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Mechanical Work:</b> Scalar dot product W = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{s} = F s cos\theta. For variable forces, W = int F(x) dx = \text{Area under } F-x \text{ curve}.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Work-Energy Theorem:</b> W_{net} = Delta K = \frac{1}{2}mv_f<sup>2</sup> - \frac{1}{2}mv_i<sup>2</sup>. Holds unconditionally for all forces.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Power:</b> Time rate of work P = dW/dt = \u000bec{F} cdot \u000bec{v} (1\text{ hp} = 746\text{ W}, 1\text{ kWh} = 3.6\text{ MJ}).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Potential Energy & Springs:</b> F = -dU/dx. Spring Hooke force F_s = -kx implies U_s = \frac{1}{2}kx<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Conservative Fields:</b> Work is path-independent, closed loop oint \u000bec{F}cdot d\u000bec{r} = 0, mechanical energy conserved E = K + U = \text{const}.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Vertical Circular Motion:</b> String loop: v_{top} = sqrt{gL}, v_{bottom} = sqrt{5gL}, v_{mid} = sqrt{3gL}, T_{bottom} - T_{top} = 6mg. Rigid rod: v_{top} = 0, v_{bottom} = sqrt{4gL}.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Collision Invariants:</b> In 1D elastic (e = 1): u_1 - u_2 = v_2 - v_1; equal masses swap velocities. In completely inelastic (e = 0): bodies stick, Delta K = \frac{1}{2}left(\frac{m_1 m_2}{m_1 + m_2}\right)(u_1 - u_2)<sup>2</sup>. In 2D elastic equal-mass collision: scattering angle \theta_1 + \theta_2 = 90<sup>c</sup>irc.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Top 5 Board Examination Trap Alerts:</b><br>\n      1. Work done by a normal force or centripetal force is strictly ZERO because \theta = 90<sup>c</sup>irc.<br>\n      2. The difference T_{bottom} - T_{top} = 6mg in vertical circles is independent of launch speed.<br>\n      3. Total linear momentum is conserved in BOTH elastic and inelastic collisions; kinetic energy is conserved ONLY in elastic collisions.<br>\n      4. A rigid rod requires only v_{top} = 0 to loop the vertical circle, whereas a string requires v_{top} = sqrt{gL} to avoid slackening.<br>\n      5. Caret symbols () are strictly forbidden in final presentation; standard superscripts must be used.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>";

